(verb
  :name "calc/jacobian" :std "Motoi"
  :library "calc"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(calc/jacobian fields point) -> matrix"
  :summary "Computes the Jacobian matrix, containing the gradient of each scalar field in a list of functions."
  :explanation "The Jacobian generalises the derivative to vector-valued functions: for a map F = (f₁, f₂, ..., fₘ) from ℝⁿ to ℝᵐ, the Jacobian is an m × n matrix where row i is ∇fᵢ. It encodes the linear approximation of F near a point. Use the Jacobian to analyse how a multivariable map behaves, to compute directional derivatives of each component, and to detect degeneracies (singular Jacobian means the map is not locally invertible)."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(define f1 (lambda (pt) (car pt)))\n(define f2 (lambda (pt) (cadr pt)))\n(calc/jacobian (list f1 f2) (list 1 2))" :note "Identity map (x, y) → (x, y); Jacobian is 2×2 identity matrix [[1,0],[0,1]]")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define fx (lambda (pt) (* (car pt) (cadr pt))))\n(define fy (lambda (pt) (+ (car pt) (cadr pt))))\n(let ((j (calc/jacobian (list fx fy) (list 2 3))))\n  (list 'row1 (car j) 'row2 (cadr j)))" :note "F(x,y)=(xy, x+y); Jacobian at (2,3) is [[∂xy/∂x, ∂xy/∂y], [∂(x+y)/∂x, ∂(x+y)/∂y]] = [[3,2],[1,1]]")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define u (lambda (pt) (* (car pt) (car pt))))\n(define v (lambda (pt) (* (cadr pt) (cadr pt))))\n(let ((fields (list u v))\n      (point (list 1 1)))\n  (let ((j (calc/jacobian fields point)))\n    (let ((det (- (* (car (car j)) (cadr (cadr j)))\n                   (* (cadr (car j)) (car (cadr j))))))\n      (if (< (abs det) 0.01) 'singular 'invertible))))" :note "Check if Jacobian is singular by testing determinant; F(x,y)=(x², y²) is singular at origin")
  )
  :caveats ("Numeric; each field's gradient is subject to finite-difference error." "Assumes fields are listed in order matching the matrix rows." "Does not compute determinant or rank; check those separately (linalg/determinant, linalg/rank)." "Very expensive for many fields or high-dimensional domains.")
  :drawbacks ("O(m·n) function evaluations for m fields and n-dimensional input; scales poorly." "If determinant or eigenvalues are needed, extract Jacobian and use linalg/* separately." "Numeric Jacobian becomes ill-conditioned near singularities.")
  :usecases ("Change of variables: verify invertibility and compute Jacobian determinant for integrals." "Implicit function theorem: check when a system can be locally solved." "Nonlinear systems: Newton's method for F(x) = 0 (vs scalar Newton with calc/newton).")
  :related ("calc/gradient" "calc/hessian" "calc/laplacian" "linalg/determinant" "linalg/rank")
  :learn (:concept "Jacobian as stacked gradients; linear approximation of vector-valued functions; invertibility." :prerequisites ("calc/gradient" "calc/directional-derivative" "linalg basics") :progression "linalg/determinant → linalg/rank → implicit function theorem (beyond this library)")
  :row-2-audit (
   
    (:problem "A change-of-variables integration or a nonlinear-solver linearization needs the Jacobian matrix of a vector-valued function."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (list (+ (car p) (cadr p)) (- (car p) (cadr p)))))\n(calc/jacobian f (list 1 1))" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "J = [[1, 1], [1, -1]]; operator sees that matrix."
     :meta "gradient is for scalar-valued functions; jacobian generalizes to vector-valued. Use jacobian when the output is a vector.")
    (:problem "A polar-to-Cartesian area-element read-out needs the Jacobian of (r, θ) → (r cos θ, r sin θ) so the operator can pick up the r-factor in the integral."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define polar (lambda (p) (let ((r (car p)) (th (cadr p))) (list (* r (cos th)) (* r (sin th))))))\n(calc/jacobian (list (lambda (p) (* (car p) (cos (cadr p)))) (lambda (p) (* (car p) (sin (cadr p))))) (list 2 0))" :tags (math program)))
     :explanation "At (r=2, θ=0) the Jacobian rows are [cos θ, −r sin θ] and [sin θ, r cos θ] = [[1,0],[0,2]]; its determinant r=2 is the area element dA = r dr dθ."
     :meta "The Jacobian determinant is precisely the volume-distortion factor that appears when you change variables inside an integral — the operator reads it straight off this matrix.")
    (:problem "A stability audit of a nonlinear system needs the Jacobian at an equilibrium so its eigenvalues can be checked; the operator supplies the field and the fixed point."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (list (- (* (car p) (car p)) 1) (- (cadr p) (car p)))))\n(define J (calc/jacobian f (list 1 1)))\n(number? (list-ref (list-ref J 0) 0))" :tags (cs program)))
     :explanation "The Jacobian of the map at the equilibrium (1,1) linearises the dynamics; feeding its entries to an eigenvalue routine classifies the fixed point as stable/unstable."
     :meta "Same matrix, different consumer: change-of-variables wants det J; dynamical-systems analysis wants the eigenvalues of J. One verb, two audits.")
    (:eng "The operator gets one line per call; failure surfaces the exact input that broke the expectation."
     :math "Category: calc-nd; the verb operates within its declared algebraic type."
     :cs "Complexity as declared in the signature; deterministic and testable."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "'best linear approximation of a vector map'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "calc native" :code "(define f (lambda (p) (list (+ (car p) (cadr p)) (- (car p) (cadr p)))))")
       (:domain "local warp matrix at a pixel" :code "(define f (lambda (p) (list (+ (car p) (cadr p)) (- (car p) (cadr p)))))\n(calc/jacobian f (list 1 1))")
       (:domain "Jacobian matrix" :code "(define polar (lambda (p) (let ((r (car p)) (th (cadr p))) (list (* r (cos th)) (* r (sin th))))))\n(calc/jacobian (list (lambda (p) (* (car p) (cos (cadr p)))) (lambda (p) (* (car p) (sin (cadr p))))) (list 2 0))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1 is calc/jacobian in its native library. Rung 2 shows the same ''best linear approximation of a vector map'' shape in a metaphor domain. Rung 3 lifts the shape into a math/logical setting where the transfer is formal. Recognizing the same shape in all three lets the reader carry the verb's intuition into problems Motoi does not directly cover."
     :meta "The shape transfers because ''best linear approximation of a vector map'' is substrate-independent — the same relationship holds whenever the underlying category admits the required structure.")
    (:eng "Engineers who have met the shape elsewhere already know when to reach for calc/jacobian."
     :math "The shape is captured by a small equation set; each rung is a model of that equation set."
     :cs "Once the shape is named, the same algorithm skeleton (traverse, close, compare) applies at each rung."))
  :row-4-proof (
   
    (:claim "For a diffeomorphism f, the volume element transforms via |det J|."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (list (+ (car p) (cadr p)) (- (car p) (cadr p)))))\n(define J (calc/jacobian f (list 0 0)))\n(number? (list-ref (list-ref J 0) 0))" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "dV(y) = |det J_f(x)| dV(x)"
     :meta "The invariant is the algebraic essence of the verb; a single counter-example would falsify it and prove the implementation broken. See book-of-math ch15-multivariable-linear-algebra. Also book-of-ml ch-03 for matrix side.")
    (:claim "The i-th row of the Jacobian equals the gradient of the i-th component field."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f1 (lambda (p) (* (car p) (cadr p))))\n(define f2 (lambda (p) (+ (car p) (cadr p))))\n(define J (calc/jacobian (list f1 f2) (list 2 3)))\n(< (abs (- (car (list-ref J 0)) (car (calc/gradient f1 (list 2 3))))) 0.05)" :tags (math program)))
     :invariant "row_i(J_F) = ∇f_i"
     :meta "The Jacobian is literally the stack of component gradients; this identity ties calc/jacobian back to calc/gradient as its scalar special case.")
    (:claim "For a linear map F(x) = Ax the Jacobian is the matrix A itself, constant everywhere."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define F (lambda (p) (list (+ (* 2 (car p)) (cadr p)) (* 3 (cadr p)))))\n(define Ja (calc/jacobian F (list 0 0)))\n(define Jb (calc/jacobian F (list 5 7)))\n(< (abs (- (car (car Ja)) (car (car Jb)))) 0.05)" :tags (cs program)))
     :invariant "F(x) = Ax ⇒ J_F(x) = A  (independent of x)"
     :meta "Linearity's fingerprint: a constant Jacobian. If J varies with x, the map has genuine curvature — the derivative is doing real work.")
    (:eng "The runnable claim doubles as a CI smoke test — if the invariant ever prints #f, the verb has drifted."
     :math "The invariant is the defining property in the calc-nd category."
     :cs "Property-based test: the program above is a runnable falsifier candidate for a QuickCheck-style fuzzer."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   
    (:base "calc/jacobian — see :summary."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (list (* (car p) (cadr p)) (+ (car p) (cadr p)))))\n(define J (calc/jacobian f (list 1 2)))\nJ" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "linearization"
     :meta "The emergent linearization belongs to the family of composed behaviours where one verb's output feeds the next verb's input, and the pair carries meaning neither verb has alone. Jacobian = best linear approximation; the multivariate analog of the tangent line.")
    (:base "calc/jacobian feeding a Newton step for a system F(x) = 0."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define F (lambda (p) (list (- (* (car p) (car p)) 2) (- (cadr p) (car p)))))\n(define J (calc/jacobian F (list 1.5 1.5)))\n(list 'residual (F (list 1.5 1.5)) 'jacobian J)" :tags (math composition)))
     :emergent "newton-system-step"
     :meta "Multivariate Newton solves J·Δx = −F(x); the Jacobian is the linear operator inverted at each iteration. This is calc/newton lifted from scalars to vectors.")
    (:base "calc/jacobian composed with a determinant to gate invertibility."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (list (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(define J (calc/jacobian f (list 0 0)))\n(define det (- (* (car (car J)) (cadr (cadr J))) (* (cadr (car J)) (car (cadr J)))))\n(if (< (abs det) 0.01) 'singular 'invertible)" :tags (cs composition)))
     :emergent "invertibility-gate"
     :meta "Jacobian → determinant → local-invertibility verdict. This is the computational heart of the inverse function theorem: a nonzero det J means the map is locally a diffeomorphism.")
    (:eng "The composition is short enough to inline in a callsite yet meaningful enough to name and reuse."
     :math "Under composition, the calc-nd operations close into the linearization pattern."
     :cs "The emergent linearization is a first-class named pattern; recognize it once and it reappears everywhere."))
)

(verb
  :name "calc/laplacian" :std "Motoi"
  :library "calc"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(calc/laplacian f point) -> number"
  :summary "Computes the Laplacian of a scalar field: the trace of the Hessian matrix, or sum of all second partial derivatives."
  :explanation "The Laplacian ∇²f = ∑ᵢ ∂²f/∂xᵢ² is a fundamental differential operator measuring how a function's value at a point differs from its average in a neighborhood. It appears in heat diffusion, wave equations, and electrostatics. Numerically, it is computed as the sum of all unmixed second partials using central differences with step h≈1e-5."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (expt (car p) 2) (expt (cadr p) 2))))\n(calc/laplacian f '(1 2))" :note "f(x,y) = x² + y² has Laplacian ∇²f = 4 everywhere; the result should be close to 4")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define f (lambda (p) (let ((x (car p)) (y (cadr p)))\n  (- (* x x) (* y y)))))\n(calc/laplacian f '(0 0))\n(calc/laplacian f '(3 4))" :note "f(x,y) = x² − y² has ∇²f = 2 − 2 = 0 everywhere; both calls should return ~0")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (field-info f region-pts)\n  (map (lambda (pt) (list pt (calc/laplacian f pt)))\n       region-pts))\n(define f (lambda (p) (expt (math/exp 1) (- (+ (expt (car p) 2) (expt (cadr p) 2))))))\n(field-info f '((0 0) (1 0) (0 1)))" :note "Computes Laplacian at multiple grid points; useful for detecting smoothness or singularities in a scalar field")
  )
  :caveats ("Requires a function that takes a list (point in ℝⁿ) and returns a scalar" "Numeric approximation; accuracy depends on step size h and function smoothness" "If f is not smooth, results may be noisy or unreliable near discontinuities" "Assumes dimension of point matches what f expects")
  :drawbacks ("Finite-difference approximation introduces truncation error O(h²)" "Expensive for high-dimensional spaces (requires 2n+1 function evaluations)" "No symbolic simplification; use calc/hessian if you need the full matrix")
  :usecases ("Detecting critical points and saddle regions in optimization" "Solving Poisson equation ∇²u = f in PDE applications" "Measuring smoothness of a neural network or interpolant" "Heat diffusion and wave equation solvers")
  :related ("calc/hessian" "calc/gradient" "calc/partial" "calc/directional-derivative" "calc/jacobian")
  :learn (:concept "The Laplacian as the sum of unmixed second derivatives; geometric meaning of curvature in all directions" :prerequisites ("calc/partial" "calc/hessian") :progression "Use calc/laplacian to understand multivariate curvature, then explore calc/jacobian for vector fields and calc/gradient for optimization")
  :row-2-audit (
   
    (:problem "A heat-equation or Poisson-solve step needs Δf = ∇·∇f at a point; the operator wants a single call rather than composing gradient and divergence."
     :programs (
       (:code "(calc/laplacian (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))) (list 0 0))" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "Δ(x² + y²) = 4; operator sees ≈ 4."
     :meta "divergence takes a vector field; laplacian takes a scalar. The two compose (Δf = div ∇f) but the direct verb is cleaner.")
    (:problem "A harmonicity screen needs to know whether a candidate potential satisfies Laplace's equation Δf = 0 before it is used as a steady-state field."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (- (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(< (abs (calc/laplacian f (list 3 4))) 0.01)" :tags (math program)))
     :explanation "Δ(x² − y²) = 2 − 2 = 0 everywhere; the check returns #t, certifying f as harmonic and usable as a potential."
     :meta "Harmonicity is the boundary case Δf = 0. The audit asks a yes/no question about a physical admissibility condition, not a magnitude.")
    (:problem "A smoothing-vs-sharpening decision on a scalar image field needs the sign of the Laplacian: positive means the point sits below its neighbourhood average."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define bump (lambda (p) (- (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p))))))\n(let ((lap (calc/laplacian bump (list 1 1)))) (if (< lap 0) 'peak 'valley))" :tags (cs program)))
     :explanation "For the downward paraboloid −(x²+y²), Δ = −4 < 0, so the point is above its neighbourhood average — a peak; the classifier returns 'peak."
     :meta "The Laplacian's sign is the mean-value comparison that drives edge-detection and diffusion kernels; the audit turns a number into a discrete label.")
    (:eng "The operator gets one line per call; failure surfaces the exact input that broke the expectation."
     :math "Category: calc-nd; the verb operates within its declared algebraic type."
     :cs "Complexity as declared in the signature; deterministic and testable."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "'trace of the Hessian'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "calc native" :code "(calc/laplacian (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))) (list 0 0))")
       (:domain "image smoothing / blur kernel" :code "(calc/laplacian (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))) (list 0 0))")
       (:domain "Δf = Σ ∂²f/∂x_i²" :code "(define f (lambda (p) (- (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(< (abs (calc/laplacian f (list 3 4))) 0.01)"))
     :explanation "Rung 1 is calc/laplacian in its native library. Rung 2 shows the same ''trace of the Hessian'' shape in a metaphor domain. Rung 3 lifts the shape into a math/logical setting where the transfer is formal. Recognizing the same shape in all three lets the reader carry the verb's intuition into problems Motoi does not directly cover."
     :meta "The shape transfers because ''trace of the Hessian'' is substrate-independent — the same relationship holds whenever the underlying category admits the required structure.")
    (:eng "Engineers who have met the shape elsewhere already know when to reach for calc/laplacian."
     :math "The shape is captured by a small equation set; each rung is a model of that equation set."
     :cs "Once the shape is named, the same algorithm skeleton (traverse, close, compare) applies at each rung."))
  :row-4-proof (
   
    (:claim "A harmonic function (Δf = 0) has no strict local extremum in the interior."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (- (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(< (abs (calc/laplacian f (list 1 1))) 0.01)" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "Δf = 0 ⇒ ∀ interior x. f(x) ∉ strict-max ∧ f(x) ∉ strict-min"
     :meta "The invariant is the algebraic essence of the verb; a single counter-example would falsify it and prove the implementation broken. See book-of-math ch15-multivariable-linear-algebra.")
    (:claim "The Laplacian equals the trace of the Hessian: Δf = Σ_i ∂²f/∂x_i²."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* 3 (* (car p) (car p))) (* 5 (* (cadr p) (cadr p))))))\n(< (abs (- (calc/laplacian f (list 1 1)) (+ (car (car (calc/hessian f (list 1 1)))) (cadr (cadr (calc/hessian f (list 1 1))))))) 0.1)" :tags (math program)))
     :invariant "Δf = tr(H_f) = Σ_i H_ii"
     :meta "The Laplacian discards the off-diagonal (mixed) second partials and keeps the diagonal — it is coordinate-free precisely because the trace is basis-invariant.")
    (:claim "The Laplacian is linear: Δ(af + bg) = a·Δf + b·Δg."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (* (car p) (car p))))\n(define g (lambda (p) (* (cadr p) (cadr p))))\n(define h (lambda (p) (+ (* 2 (f p)) (* 3 (g p)))))\n(< (abs (- (calc/laplacian h (list 1 1)) (+ (* 2 (calc/laplacian f (list 1 1))) (* 3 (calc/laplacian g (list 1 1)))))) 0.05)" :tags (cs program)))
     :invariant "Δ(af + bg) = a Δf + b Δg"
     :meta "Linearity is why superposition works for the heat and Poisson equations — solutions add. A counter-example here would break every PDE solver built on the operator.")
    (:eng "The runnable claim doubles as a CI smoke test — if the invariant ever prints #f, the verb has drifted."
     :math "The invariant is the defining property in the calc-nd category."
     :cs "Property-based test: the program above is a runnable falsifier candidate for a QuickCheck-style fuzzer."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   
    (:base "calc/laplacian — see :summary."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(list (calc/laplacian f (list 0 0)) (calc/hessian f (list 0 0)))" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "diffusion-operator"
     :meta "The emergent diffusion-operator belongs to the family of composed behaviours where one verb's output feeds the next verb's input, and the pair carries meaning neither verb has alone. Laplacian appears in heat, wave, and diffusion equations. See book-of-ml ch-01.")
    (:base "calc/laplacian sampled across a grid to detect sources and sinks."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(map (lambda (pt) (list pt (calc/laplacian f pt))) (list (list 0 0) (list 1 0) (list 2 2)))" :tags (math composition)))
     :emergent "poisson-residual-field"
     :meta "Evaluating Δf over a grid yields the right-hand side ρ of Poisson's equation Δf = ρ; a nonzero Laplacian marks a source/sink. This is the sampling loop inside every field solver.")
    (:base "calc/laplacian composed with calc/gradient to reconstruct div∘grad."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* 2 (* (cadr p) (cadr p))))))\n(define pt (list 1 1))\n(list 'laplacian (calc/laplacian f pt) 'gradient (calc/gradient f pt))" :tags (cs composition)))
     :emergent "div-grad-identity"
     :meta "Δf = ∇·(∇f): the composition exposes the gradient field whose divergence the Laplacian silently takes. Naming the pair makes the vector-calculus identity operational.")
    (:eng "The composition is short enough to inline in a callsite yet meaningful enough to name and reuse."
     :math "Under composition, the calc-nd operations close into the diffusion-operator pattern."
     :cs "The emergent diffusion-operator is a first-class named pattern; recognize it once and it reappears everywhere."))
)

(verb
  :name "calc/limit" :std "Motoi"
  :library "calc"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(calc/limit f a) -> number"
  :summary "Computes the two-sided numeric limit of a univariate function f as x approaches a."
  :explanation "A limit describes the value a function approaches as its argument gets arbitrarily close to a point. This verb uses numeric sampling: it evaluates f at points approaching a from both left and right, and returns a deterministic float. It answers 'what does f(x) approach as x→a?' without requiring symbolic form. Useful for checking continuity and finding asymptotic behavior."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(calc/limit (lambda (x) (/ (- (* x x) 4) (- x 2))) 2)" :note "The function (x²−4)/(x−2) = (x+2)(x−2)/(x−2) approaches 4 as x→2; result ≈ 4")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define f (lambda (x) (/ (- 1 (cos x)) (* x x))))\n(calc/limit f 0)" :note "As x→0, (1−cos x)/x² approaches 0.5 (important in Taylor series); result ≈ 0.5")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (check-removable-singularity f a)\n  (list 'limit-at a (calc/limit f a))\n  (list 'nearby-values (map (lambda (h) (f (+ a h)))\n                            '(0.1 0.01 0.001))))\n(check-removable-singularity (lambda (x) (/ (sin x) x)) 0)" :note "Tests whether a singularity is removable by checking if limit exists and differs from nearby samples")
  )
  :caveats ("Numeric approximation; detects limits only if left and right sequences converge to same value" "May fail if f oscillates wildly near a (e.g., sin(1/x) as x→0)" "Returns a single deterministic float, not a proof; always verify with symbolic reasoning if critical" "Does not detect infinite limits; may return large number or diverge")
  :drawbacks ("Slower than symbolic limit computation if available" "Cannot distinguish between nonexistent and infinite limits without external checking" "Accuracy depends on function smoothness and numerical stability near a")
  :usecases ("Testing continuity of piecewise functions at transition points" "Estimating asymptotic behavior where symbolic form is unknown" "Verifying Taylor series and approximations" "Checking removable singularities (holes in graphs)")
  :related ("calc/derivative" "calc/continuous?" "calc/differentiable?" "calc/taylor")
  :learn (:concept "Numeric estimation of limiting behavior; understanding continuity via limits" :prerequisites () :progression "Use calc/limit to check continuity, then calc/derivative to measure instantaneous rate of change")
  :row-2-audit (
   
    (:problem "A hole in a rational function needs its limit value; the operator wants a numerical approximation without the algebraic manipulation."
     :programs (
       (:code "(calc/limit (lambda (x) (/ (- (* x x) 1) (- x 1))) 1)" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "Limit of (x²-1)/(x-1) at 1 is 2; operator sees ≈ 2."
     :meta "continuous? asks whether the function is defined AND continuous; limit asks 'what value would fill the hole?' — a weaker but related question.")
    (:problem "A Taylor-coefficient sanity check needs a fundamental trig limit that underlies the derivative of sine at 0."
     :programs (
       (:code "(< (abs (- (calc/limit (lambda (x) (/ (sin x) x)) 0) 1)) 0.01)" :tags (math program)))
     :explanation "lim_{x→0} sin(x)/x = 1; the check returns #t, confirming the standard limit that makes (sin x)' = cos x."
     :meta "This is the archetypal 0/0 indeterminate form; the operator relies on it whenever a derivative is defined as a difference quotient's limit.")
    (:problem "A rate-of-approach read-out needs the derivative-as-limit definition to be evaluated directly, so the operator can verify an autodiff result against first principles."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (* x x)))\n(define diff-quotient (lambda (h) (/ (- (f (+ 2 h)) (f 2)) h)))\n(< (abs (- (calc/limit diff-quotient 0) 4)) 0.05)" :tags (cs program)))
     :explanation "The limit of the difference quotient of x² at x=2 is f'(2) = 4; the operator recovers the derivative straight from its definition."
     :meta "limit is the primitive under calc/derivative; running it on a difference quotient is the ground-truth cross-check for any numerical differentiator.")
    (:eng "The operator gets one line per call; failure surfaces the exact input that broke the expectation."
     :math "Category: calc-1d; the verb operates within its declared algebraic type."
     :cs "Complexity as declared in the signature; deterministic and testable."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "'value approached by a function'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "calc native" :code "(calc/limit (lambda (x) (/ (- (* x x) 1) (- x 1))) 1)")
       (:domain "convergence of an iterative computation" :code "(calc/limit (lambda (x) (/ (- (* x x) 1) (- x 1))) 1)")
       (:domain "lim_{x → a} f(x)" :code "(calc/limit (lambda (x) (/ (- (* x x) 1) (- x 1))) 1)"))
     :explanation "Rung 1 is calc/limit in its native library. Rung 2 shows the same ''value approached by a function'' shape in a metaphor domain. Rung 3 lifts the shape into a math/logical setting where the transfer is formal. Recognizing the same shape in all three lets the reader carry the verb's intuition into problems Motoi does not directly cover."
     :meta "The shape transfers because ''value approached by a function'' is substrate-independent — the same relationship holds whenever the underlying category admits the required structure.")
    (:eng "Engineers who have met the shape elsewhere already know when to reach for calc/limit."
     :math "The shape is captured by a small equation set; each rung is a model of that equation set."
     :cs "Once the shape is named, the same algorithm skeleton (traverse, close, compare) applies at each rung."))
  :row-4-proof (
   
    (:claim "Uniqueness: if lim f exists, it is unique."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (/ (- (* x x) 1) (- x 1))))\n(define L1 (calc/limit f 1))\n(define L2 (calc/limit f 1))\n(< (abs (- L1 L2)) 0.001)" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "∀ f, a. lim_{x → a} f(x) exists ⇒ unique"
     :meta "The invariant is the algebraic essence of the verb; a single counter-example would falsify it and prove the implementation broken. See book-of-math ch13-calculus-1 and ch14-calculus-2.")
    (:claim "The limit is linear: lim (a·f + b·g) = a·lim f + b·lim g when both limits exist."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (/ (sin x) x)))\n(define g (lambda (x) (/ (- 1 (cos x)) (* x x))))\n(< (abs (- (calc/limit (lambda (x) (+ (* 2 (f x)) (* 4 (g x)))) 0) (+ (* 2 1) (* 4 0.5)))) 0.05)" :tags (math program)))
     :invariant "lim (a f + b g) = a lim f + b lim g"
     :meta "Linearity of the limit is what lets you split a complicated limit into simpler pieces — the algebraic backbone of every limit-computation rule.")
    (:claim "Continuity is the fixed point of the limit: if f is continuous at a, lim_{x→a} f(x) = f(a)."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (+ (* 3 x) 1)))\n(< (abs (- (calc/limit f 2) (f 2))) 0.001)" :tags (cs program)))
     :invariant "f continuous at a ⇔ lim_{x→a} f(x) = f(a)"
     :meta "For continuous functions the limit is just evaluation; the interesting cases (holes, jumps) are exactly where this identity fails. This is calc/continuous? stated as a limit law.")
    (:eng "The runnable claim doubles as a CI smoke test — if the invariant ever prints #f, the verb has drifted."
     :math "The invariant is the defining property in the calc-1d category."
     :cs "Property-based test: the program above is a runnable falsifier candidate for a QuickCheck-style fuzzer."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   
    (:base "calc/limit — see :summary."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (/ (sin x) x)))\n(list (calc/limit f 0) (calc/derivative f 1))" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "removable-singularity-fill"
     :meta "The emergent removable-singularity-fill belongs to the family of composed behaviours where one verb's output feeds the next verb's input, and the pair carries meaning neither verb has alone. Limit + define-by-cases = the closure operation on partial functions.")
    (:base "calc/limit applied to the difference quotient to synthesise the derivative."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (* x x x)))\n(define at (lambda (a) (calc/limit (lambda (h) (/ (- (f (+ a h)) (f a)) h)) 0)))\n(map at (list 1 2 3))" :tags (math composition)))
     :emergent "derivative-from-limit"
     :meta "Partially applying the base point turns the limit of a difference quotient into the derivative function itself — this composition builds calc/derivative from calc/limit primitives.")
    (:base "calc/limit sweeping a family of holes to build a continuous extension."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define holes (list (lambda (x) (/ (- (* x x) 4) (- x 2))) (lambda (x) (/ (- (* x x) 9) (- x 3)))))\n(list (calc/limit (car holes) 2) (calc/limit (cadr holes) 3))" :tags (cs composition)))
     :emergent "continuous-extension"
     :meta "Mapping calc/limit over a batch of removable singularities yields the fill values that patch each hole; the emergent pattern is the continuous extension of a partial function.")
    (:eng "The composition is short enough to inline in a callsite yet meaningful enough to name and reuse."
     :math "Under composition, the calc-1d operations close into the removable-singularity-fill pattern."
     :cs "The emergent removable-singularity-fill is a first-class named pattern; recognize it once and it reappears everywhere."))
)

(verb
  :name "calc/line-integral" :std "Motoi"
  :library "calc"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(calc/line-integral f-or-F curve [integrand-type]) -> scalar"
  :summary "Computes the line integral of a scalar or vector field over a closed curve using deterministic Simpson's rule."
  :explanation "A line integral integrates a field along a path. For a scalar field f, the integral is ∫f ds (arc length); for a vector field F, it is ∫F·dr (dot product of field and path tangent). calc/line-integral takes a field (lambda) and a curve closure (a parameterized path) and returns the integrated result. The integration uses Simpson's rule, which is deterministic and consistent across runs."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(define circle (lambda (t) (list (cos (* 2 3.14159 t)) (sin (* 2 3.14159 t)) 0)))\n(define f (lambda (x y z) 1))\n(define arc-len (calc/line-integral f circle))\n(display arc-len)" :note "Integrate constant field 1 over a unit circle; result ≈ 2π")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define line (lambda (t) (list (* t 10) 0 0)))\n(define F (lambda (x y z) (list 1 0 0)))\n(define work (calc/line-integral F line 'vector))\n(display (list \"Work done:\" work))" :note "Compute work (line integral of force) along a straight path")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (circulation-in-field F)\n  (let ((circle (lambda (t)\n    (list (* 5 (cos (* 2 3.14159 t)))\n          (* 5 (sin (* 2 3.14159 t)))\n          0))))\n    (calc/line-integral F circle 'vector)))\n(define curl-field (lambda (x y z) (list (- y) x 0)))\n(let ((circ (circulation-in-field curl-field)))\n  (display (list \"Circulation (should be ≈ 50π):\" circ)))" :note "Compute circulation of a rotational field around a circle; should relate to curl")
  )
  :caveats ("Curve closure must return a 3-element list [x, y, z] for each parameter t ∈ [0, 1]." "Integration type ('scalar or 'vector) must match the field; scalar field with 'vector mode will misinterpret results." "Simpson's rule is accurate for smooth fields; discontinuous or very oscillatory fields may be approximated coarsely." "Result is deterministic within the device but subject to floating-point precision.")
  :drawbacks ("Fixed quadrature order (Simpson); no adaptive refinement for highly oscillatory fields." "Curve parameterization must be smooth; discontinuous jumps in the curve will produce spurious results." "No symbolic simplification; calling repeatedly for the same curve/field is inefficient." "Curve must be a closure; cannot directly pass analytic equations.")
  :usecases ("Physics: compute work done by a force along a trajectory." "Electromagnetism: evaluate Faraday's law ∮E·dr around a loop." "Fluid dynamics: measure circulation ∮v·dr around a vortex." "Complex analysis: integrate functions along contours (if restricted to 2-D).")
  :related ("calc/surface-integral" "calc/curl" "calc/divergence" "num/nintegrate-2d" "num/nintegrate-3d")
  :learn (:concept "Line integrals extend the idea of integration to curves in space; they measure the 'accumulation' of a field along a path." :prerequisites ("calculus / parametric curves" "lambda (to define fields and curves)") :progression "After calc/line-integral, explore calc/surface-integral to integrate over 2-D surfaces, and use both with Stokes' and divergence theorems.")
  :row-2-audit (
   
    (:problem "A work-energy computation over a curved path needs ∫_γ F·dr; the operator supplies the path and the field and expects a scalar."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define F (lambda (p) (list (car p) (cadr p))))\n(define x (lambda (t) t)) (define y (lambda (t) t))\n(calc/line-integral F x y 0 1)" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "F = (x, y) along y = x from 0 to 1; ∫ = 1; operator sees ≈ 1."
     :meta "integral is 1-D over an interval; line-integral is 1-D over a curve in higher dimensions. Same idea, different geometry.")
    (:problem "A perimeter measurement needs the arc-length line integral ∫ 1 ds of the constant field over a closed curve."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define circle (lambda (t) (list (cos (* 2 3.14159 t)) (sin (* 2 3.14159 t)) 0)))\n(define one (lambda (x y z) 1))\n(< (abs (- (calc/line-integral one circle) (* 2 3.14159))) 0.1)" :tags (math program)))
     :explanation "Integrating the scalar 1 along the unit circle returns its circumference 2π ≈ 6.283; the operator reads perimeter directly."
     :meta "The scalar mode (∫ f ds) measures a geometric quantity of the path; the vector mode (∫ F·dr) measures work. Same verb, two integrand types.")
    (:problem "A circulation read-out needs ∮ F·dr of a rotational field around a loop, to compare against curl by Stokes' theorem."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define swirl (lambda (x y z) (list (- y) x 0)))\n(define circle (lambda (t) (list (cos (* 2 3.14159 t)) (sin (* 2 3.14159 t)) 0)))\n(number? (calc/line-integral swirl circle 'vector))" :tags (cs program)))
     :explanation "The line integral of the swirl field (−y, x, 0) around the unit circle returns its circulation 2π; the operator confirms a numeric result to feed the Stokes check."
     :meta "Circulation is a closed-loop line integral; its value equals the curl flux through the enclosed disk — the audit surfaces the number both sides must agree on.")
    (:eng "The operator gets one line per call; failure surfaces the exact input that broke the expectation."
     :math "Category: calc-nd; the verb operates within its declared algebraic type."
     :cs "Complexity as declared in the signature; deterministic and testable."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "'path integral of a field'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "calc native" :code "(define F (lambda (p) (list (car p) (cadr p))))")
       (:domain "work done along a sprite trajectory" :code "(define F (lambda (p) (list (car p) (cadr p))))\n(define x (lambda (t) t)) (define y (lambda (t) t))\n(calc/line-integral F x y 0 1)")
       (:domain "∫_γ F · dr" :code "(define F (lambda (x y z) (list 1 0 0)))\n(define line (lambda (t) (list (* t 10) 0 0)))\n(calc/line-integral F line 'vector)"))
     :explanation "Rung 1 is calc/line-integral in its native library. Rung 2 shows the same ''path integral of a field'' shape in a metaphor domain. Rung 3 lifts the shape into a math/logical setting where the transfer is formal. Recognizing the same shape in all three lets the reader carry the verb's intuition into problems Motoi does not directly cover."
     :meta "The shape transfers because ''path integral of a field'' is substrate-independent — the same relationship holds whenever the underlying category admits the required structure.")
    (:eng "Engineers who have met the shape elsewhere already know when to reach for calc/line-integral."
     :math "The shape is captured by a small equation set; each rung is a model of that equation set."
     :cs "Once the shape is named, the same algorithm skeleton (traverse, close, compare) applies at each rung."))
  :row-4-proof (
   
    (:claim "For a conservative field F = ∇φ, the line integral depends only on endpoints (path independence)."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define phi (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(define F (lambda (p) (calc/gradient phi p)))\n(define x1 (lambda (t) t)) (define y1 (lambda (t) t))\n(define x2 (lambda (t) (* t t))) (define y2 (lambda (t) t))\n(< (abs (- (calc/line-integral F x1 y1 0 1) (calc/line-integral F x2 y2 0 1))) 0.05)" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "F = ∇φ ⇒ ∫_γ F · dr = φ(γ(b)) - φ(γ(a))"
     :meta "The invariant is the algebraic essence of the verb; a single counter-example would falsify it and prove the implementation broken. See book-of-math ch15-multivariable-linear-algebra.")
    (:claim "The line integral of a conservative field around any closed loop is zero."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define F (lambda (p) (list (* 2 (car p)) (* 2 (cadr p)))))\n(define loop (lambda (t) (list (cos (* 2 3.14159 t)) (sin (* 2 3.14159 t)) 0)))\n(< (abs (calc/line-integral F loop 'vector)) 0.1)" :tags (math program)))
     :invariant "F = ∇φ ⇒ ∮_γ F · dr = 0"
     :meta "Zero circulation is the closed-loop face of path independence: with the same endpoints the endpoint difference φ(a)−φ(a) vanishes. A nonzero result would prove the field non-conservative.")
    (:claim "Reversing the orientation of the curve negates the line integral."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define F (lambda (p) (list (car p) (cadr p) 0)))\n(define fwd (lambda (t) (list t t 0)))\n(define rev (lambda (t) (list (- 1 t) (- 1 t) 0)))\n(< (abs (+ (calc/line-integral F fwd 'vector) (calc/line-integral F rev 'vector))) 0.05)" :tags (cs program)))
     :invariant "∫_{-γ} F · dr = -∫_γ F · dr"
     :meta "Orientation carries a sign just as interval reversal does for calc/integral; this is why oriented line integrals glue consistently along a subdivided path."
     )
    (:eng "The runnable claim doubles as a CI smoke test — if the invariant ever prints #f, the verb has drifted."
     :math "The invariant is the defining property in the calc-nd category."
     :cs "Property-based test: the program above is a runnable falsifier candidate for a QuickCheck-style fuzzer."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   
    (:base "calc/line-integral — see :summary."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define F (lambda (p) (list (- (cadr p)) (car p))))\n(define x (lambda (t) (cos t))) (define y (lambda (t) (sin t)))\n(calc/line-integral F x y 0 (* 2 3.14159))" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "circulation"
     :meta "The emergent circulation belongs to the family of composed behaviours where one verb's output feeds the next verb's input, and the pair carries meaning neither verb has alone. Circulation = line integral around a closed loop; connected to curl by Stokes. See book-of-ml ch-01.")
    (:base "calc/line-integral paired with calc/curl to witness Stokes' theorem."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define F (lambda (p) (list (- (cadr p)) (car p) 0)))\n(define circle (lambda (t) (list (cos (* 2 3.14159 t)) (sin (* 2 3.14159 t)) 0)))\n(list 'circulation (calc/line-integral F circle 'vector) 'curl-z (caddr (calc/curl F (list 0 0 0))))" :tags (math composition)))
     :emergent "stokes-witness"
     :meta "Circulation around the loop should equal the curl flux through the enclosed disk; running both sides side by side turns Stokes' theorem into a runnable identity."
     )
    (:base "calc/line-integral fed a gradient field to recover a potential difference."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define phi (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(define F (lambda (p) (calc/gradient phi p)))\n(define path (lambda (t) (list t (* t t) 0)))\n(list 'work (calc/line-integral F path 'vector) 'potential-diff (- (phi (list 1 1)) (phi (list 0 0))))" :tags (cs composition)))
     :emergent "potential-difference"
     :meta "Line-integrating a gradient field recovers φ(end)−φ(start); the composition demonstrates the gradient theorem, the 1-D face of the fundamental theorem for vector fields."
     )
    (:eng "The composition is short enough to inline in a callsite yet meaningful enough to name and reuse."
     :math "Under composition, the calc-nd operations close into the circulation pattern."
     :cs "The emergent circulation is a first-class named pattern; recognize it once and it reappears everywhere."))
)

(verb
  :name "calc/newton" :std "Motoi"
  :library "calc"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(calc/newton f x0 [tolerance iterations]) -> number"
  :summary "Finds a root of f using Newton's method, starting from initial guess x0."
  :explanation "Newton's method is an iterative algorithm: given a starting point x₀, repeatedly compute xₙ₊₁ = xₙ − f(xₙ)/f'(xₙ) until convergence. It converges rapidly (quadratic) near simple roots, but may diverge if started far away or near inflection points. This verb automates the iteration and returns the estimated root as a deterministic float."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(calc/newton (lambda (x) (- (* x x) 2)) 1.5)" :note "Finds a root of f(x) = x² − 2 (which is √2 ≈ 1.414); starting from 1.5 converges in few steps")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define f (lambda (x) (- (math/exp x) 3)))\n(define root (calc/newton f 1))\n(list 'root root 'check (f root))" :note "Finds root of eˣ = 3, which is ln(3); result ≈ 1.0986, and f(root) ≈ 0")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (find-roots-from-multiple-starts f starts)\n  (define (safe-newton x0)\n    (if (> (abs (calc/derivative f x0)) 0.01)\n        (calc/newton f x0)\n        '*diverged*))\n  (map safe-newton starts))\n(find-roots-from-multiple-starts (lambda (x) (- (* x x x) (* 2 x) 3))\n                                 '(0.5 1.5 2.5))" :note "Tries Newton from multiple starting points, filtering out cases where derivative is too small (risking division)")
  )
  :caveats ("Requires a nonzero derivative near the root; will fail if f'(x) ≈ 0" "Convergence is local: must start reasonably close to root (basin of attraction varies)" "May cycle or diverge if f has inflection points or multiple roots nearby" "Finds only one root; to find all roots, try multiple starting points" "Does not detect if root is a multiple root (converges slower there)")
  :drawbacks ("Requires computing f'(x) numerically at each iteration (2+ function calls per step)" "Sensitive to initial guess; poor choice leads to divergence or wrong root" "Does not bracket the root; no guaranteed convergence without good starting point" "Cannot find complex roots (only real-valued here)")
  :usecases ("Solving nonlinear equations where algebraic solution is impractical" "Finding equilibrium points in dynamical systems" "Solving implicit equations f(x) = c by shifting to f(x) − c = 0" "Machine learning: root-finding in optimization (alternative to gradient descent)")
  :related ("calc/derivative" "calc/limit" "calc/tangent-line")
  :learn (:concept "Iterative refinement using linear approximation (tangent line); quadratic convergence" :prerequisites ("calc/derivative" "calc/tangent-line") :progression "Master Newton for univariate roots, then explore multidimensional Newton via calc/jacobian")
  :row-2-audit (
   
    (:problem "A root-finding problem for a smooth f needs a fast iteration; Newton's method converges quadratically near a simple root."
     :programs (
       (:code "(calc/newton (lambda (x) (- (* x x) 2)) 1.5)" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "√2 ≈ 1.4142; operator sees a value very close to it."
     :meta "critical-points-1d finds where f' = 0; newton finds where f = 0. Different targets.")
    (:problem "An implicit-equation solve of the form g(x) = c needs to be rewritten as g(x) − c = 0 so Newton can chase the crossing."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define g (lambda (x) (math/exp x)))\n(define root (calc/newton (lambda (x) (- (g x) 3)) 1))\n(< (abs (- root (log 3))) 0.001)" :tags (math program)))
     :explanation "Solving eˣ = 3 becomes finding the root of eˣ − 3; Newton returns ln 3 ≈ 1.0986."
     :meta "Any equation g(x) = c is a root problem in disguise (g(x) − c = 0); this shift is the operator's standard move before calling calc/newton.")
    (:problem "A reciprocal computation without a divide instruction needs the root of f(x) = 1/x − a, which Newton solves using only multiplies."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define a 7)\n(define root (calc/newton (lambda (x) (- (/ 1.0 x) a)) 0.1))\n(< (abs (- root (/ 1.0 a))) 0.001)" :tags (cs program)))
     :explanation "The root of 1/x − 7 is 1/7 ≈ 0.1429; Newton's update x(2 − a·x) needs no division, which is how hardware computes reciprocals."
     :meta "Newton is not only a solver but a construction: it derives division-free reciprocal and square-root algorithms used in real numeric libraries."
     )
    (:eng "The operator gets one line per call; failure surfaces the exact input that broke the expectation."
     :math "Category: calc-1d; the verb operates within its declared algebraic type."
     :cs "Complexity as declared in the signature; deterministic and testable."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "'iterative root-finder using tangent lines'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "calc native" :code "(calc/newton (lambda (x) (- (* x x) 2)) 1.5)")
       (:domain "binary-search refinement of a fixed point" :code "(calc/newton (lambda (x) (- (* x x) 2)) 1.5)")
       (:domain "Newton iteration" :code "(calc/newton (lambda (x) (- (* x x) 2)) 1.5)"))
     :explanation "Rung 1 is calc/newton in its native library. Rung 2 shows the same ''iterative root-finder using tangent lines'' shape in a metaphor domain. Rung 3 lifts the shape into a math/logical setting where the transfer is formal. Recognizing the same shape in all three lets the reader carry the verb's intuition into problems Motoi does not directly cover."
     :meta "The shape transfers because ''iterative root-finder using tangent lines'' is substrate-independent — the same relationship holds whenever the underlying category admits the required structure.")
    (:eng "Engineers who have met the shape elsewhere already know when to reach for calc/newton."
     :math "The shape is captured by a small equation set; each rung is a model of that equation set."
     :cs "Once the shape is named, the same algorithm skeleton (traverse, close, compare) applies at each rung."))
  :row-4-proof (
   
    (:claim "Near a simple root, Newton's iteration converges quadratically: |x_{n+1} - r| ≤ C |x_n - r|²."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (- (* x x) 2)))\n(define r (calc/newton f 1.5))\n(< (abs (f r)) 0.0001)" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "Near simple root r: |x_{n+1} - r| ≤ C |x_n - r|²"
     :meta "The invariant is the algebraic essence of the verb; a single counter-example would falsify it and prove the implementation broken. See book-of-math ch-advanced-10 (Numerical Methods).")
    (:claim "A converged Newton estimate is a fixed point of the Newton map N(x) = x - f(x)/f-prime(x)."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (- (* x x x) x 2)))\n(define r (calc/newton f 1.5))\n(define (fp x) (calc/derivative f x))\n(< (abs (- r (- r (/ (f r) (fp r))))) 0.001)" :tags (math program)))
     :invariant "r = N(r) ⇔ f(r) = 0  where N(x) = x − f(x)/f'(x)"
     :meta "Newton's method is fixed-point iteration on N; the returned root is precisely where the correction term f(r)/f'(r) vanishes, i.e. where f(r) = 0."
     )
    (:claim "The returned value actually satisfies the equation: f(root) ≈ 0 within tolerance."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (- (math/exp x) (* 2 x) 1)))\n(define r (calc/newton f 1.5))\n(< (abs (f r)) 0.001)" :tags (cs program)))
     :invariant "root = newton(f, x0) ⇒ |f(root)| < tol"
     :meta "The most basic contract: the output is a root. A property-based fuzzer feeds random smooth f and starting points and asserts the residual is small whenever Newton returns a number."
     )
    (:eng "The runnable claim doubles as a CI smoke test — if the invariant ever prints #f, the verb has drifted."
     :math "The invariant is the defining property in the calc-1d category."
     :cs "Property-based test: the program above is a runnable falsifier candidate for a QuickCheck-style fuzzer."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   
    (:base "calc/newton — see :summary."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (- (* x x x) x 1)))\n(define seeds (list 1 -1 0.5))\n(map (lambda (s) (calc/newton f s)) seeds)" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "root-finder"
     :meta "The emergent root-finder belongs to the family of composed behaviours where one verb's output feeds the next verb's input, and the pair carries meaning neither verb has alone. Newton is the archetype quadratically-convergent iteration. See book-of-ml ch-06 for the descent-analog.")
    (:base "calc/newton driven on a derivative to locate a critical point."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (+ (* x x x x) (- (* 2 x x)) 1)))\n(define (fp x) (calc/derivative f x))\n(define crit (calc/newton fp 1.5))\n(list 'critical-x crit 'f-there (f crit))" :tags (math composition)))
     :emergent "optimizer-step"
     :meta "Feeding f' to Newton finds where f' = 0, i.e. a stationary point of f; this is Newton's method for optimization — the second-order analogue of gradient descent."
     )
    (:base "calc/newton composed with calc/second-derivative to classify the found stationary point."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (- (* x x) (* 4 x))))\n(define (fp x) (calc/derivative f x))\n(define crit (calc/newton fp 0))\n(if (> (calc/second-derivative f crit) 0) 'minimum 'maximum)" :tags (cs composition)))
     :emergent "classified-extremum"
     :meta "Root-of-derivative locates the stationary point; the second-derivative test labels it. The composition is the full univariate optimizer that calc/extrema-1d automates."
     )
    (:eng "The composition is short enough to inline in a callsite yet meaningful enough to name and reuse."
     :math "Under composition, the calc-1d operations close into the root-finder pattern."
     :cs "The emergent root-finder is a first-class named pattern; recognize it once and it reappears everywhere."))
)

(verb
  :name "calc/partial" :std "Motoi"
  :library "calc"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(calc/partial f i point) -> number"
  :summary "Computes the i-th partial derivative ∂f/∂xᵢ at a given point using central differences."
  :explanation "A partial derivative measures how a multivariable function changes when one variable changes while others stay fixed. This verb evaluates the derivative with respect to the i-th coordinate: perturbs point by small step h in that coordinate only, then applies the central-difference formula. Returns a scalar float deterministically, making it building-block for gradient, Hessian, Laplacian, and Jacobian."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* 2 (car p)) (expt (cadr p) 2))))\n(calc/partial f 0 '(3 4))\n(calc/partial f 1 '(3 4))" :note "f(x,y) = 2x + y²; ∂f/∂x = 2 everywhere, ∂f/∂y = 2y; results ≈ 2 and ≈ 8")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define f (lambda (p) (* (car p) (cadr p))))\n(define pt '(2 3))\n(list 'partial-x (calc/partial f 0 pt)\n      'partial-y (calc/partial f 1 pt))" :note "f(x,y) = xy; ∂f/∂x = y, ∂f/∂y = x; at (2,3) results ≈ 3 and ≈ 2")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (numeric-gradient f n point)\n  (map (lambda (i) (calc/partial f i point))\n       (iota n)))\n(define f (lambda (x y z) (+ (* x x) (* y y) (* z z))))\n(numeric-gradient f 3 '(1 2 3))" :note "Builds full gradient numerically; f(x,y,z) = x²+y²+z² has gradient (2x, 2y, 2z) = (2, 4, 6) at (1,2,3); f takes individual args")
  )
  :caveats ("Index i must be valid for the point (0-indexed, less than length)" "Numeric approximation; assumes f is smooth enough near point" "Step size h is fixed at ~1e-5; may be too large or small for extremely flat/steep functions" "Point must be a list of numbers; f must accept and return a number")
  :drawbacks ("Requires three function evaluations (center, forward, backward) per partial" "Becomes expensive for high-dimensional optimization (n partials = 3n function calls)" "Central difference is O(h²) accurate but requires h small (affects condition number)" "Does not detect or warn about discontinuities")
  :usecases ("Building blocks for gradients (calc/gradient) and optimization algorithms" "Verifying symbolic derivatives numerically" "Computing sensitivities: how output changes with respect to each input" "Estimating heat flow or chemical concentrations in PDEs")
  :related ("calc/gradient" "calc/hessian" "calc/laplacian" "calc/directional-derivative" "calc/jacobian")
  :learn (:concept "Partial derivatives as directional change along coordinate axes; central-difference approximation" :prerequisites ("calc/derivative") :progression "Master calc/partial, then use it with calc/gradient and calc/hessian for optimization")
  :row-2-audit (
   
    (:problem "A partial-derivative-with-respect-to-one-variable is what backpropagation needs to build the gradient component-by-component."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(calc/partial f 0 (list 1 2))" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "∂f/∂x at (1, 2) = 2x = 2; operator sees ≈ 2."
     :meta "gradient is the full vector; partial is one component. Use partial when you don't need the whole vector.")
    (:problem "A sensitivity report needs ∂output/∂input for one specific parameter while all others are held fixed, so the operator can rank which input matters most."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define profit (lambda (p) (- (* 3 (car p)) (* 2 (cadr p)) (* (caddr p) (caddr p)))))\n(list (calc/partial profit 0 (list 4 5 2)) (calc/partial profit 2 (list 4 5 2)))" :tags (math program)))
     :explanation "∂profit/∂p0 = 3 (constant sensitivity) and ∂profit/∂p2 = −2·2 = −4 at the operating point; the operator sees (3 −4) and knows p2 hurts twice as fast."
     :meta "A partial answers 'if I nudge only this knob, how does the output respond?' — the local sensitivity that drives what-if analysis."
     )
    (:problem "A mixed-partial computation needs to differentiate first in x then in y, so the operator nests two partial calls."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (* (car p) (car p) (cadr p))))\n(define fx (lambda (q) (calc/partial f 0 q)))\n(< (abs (- (calc/partial fx 1 (list 2 3)) 4)) 0.1)" :tags (cs program)))
     :explanation "∂²(x²y)/∂x∂y = ∂(2xy)/∂y = 2x = 4 at (2,3); nesting partials builds the mixed derivative the operator wants."
     :meta "Because calc/partial takes and returns numeric-at-a-point maps, it composes with itself to reach any higher mixed partial — the building block of the Hessian's off-diagonal entries."
     )
    (:eng "The operator gets one line per call; failure surfaces the exact input that broke the expectation."
     :math "Category: calc-nd; the verb operates within its declared algebraic type."
     :cs "Complexity as declared in the signature; deterministic and testable."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "'axis-aligned rate of change'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "calc native" :code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))")
       (:domain "per-channel color derivative" :code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(calc/partial f 0 (list 1 2))")
       (:domain "∂f/∂x_i" :code "(define (finite-diff f x h)\n  (/ (- (f (+ x h)) (f (- x h))) (* 2 h)))\n(define f (lambda (x) (* x x x)))\n(list (finite-diff f 1 0.01)\n      (calc/derivative f 1))\n;; both should give ≈ 3.0 (d/dx x³ at x=1)"))
     :explanation "Rung 1 is calc/partial in its native library. Rung 2 shows the same ''axis-aligned rate of change'' shape in a metaphor domain. Rung 3 lifts the shape into a math/logical setting where the transfer is formal. Recognizing the same shape in all three lets the reader carry the verb's intuition into problems Motoi does not directly cover."
     :meta "The shape transfers because ''axis-aligned rate of change'' is substrate-independent — the same relationship holds whenever the underlying category admits the required structure.")
    (:eng "Engineers who have met the shape elsewhere already know when to reach for calc/partial."
     :math "The shape is captured by a small equation set; each rung is a model of that equation set."
     :cs "Once the shape is named, the same algorithm skeleton (traverse, close, compare) applies at each rung."))
  :row-4-proof (
   
    (:claim "For f ∈ C^2, the mixed partials commute: ∂²f/∂x∂y = ∂²f/∂y∂x."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (* (car p) (cadr p))))\n(define fx (lambda (p) (calc/partial f 0 p)))\n(define fxy (lambda (p) (calc/partial fx 1 p)))\n(define fy (lambda (p) (calc/partial f 1 p)))\n(define fyx (lambda (p) (calc/partial fy 0 p)))\n(< (abs (- (fxy (list 1 1)) (fyx (list 1 1)))) 0.05)" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "f ∈ C^2 ⇒ ∂²f/∂x∂y = ∂²f/∂y∂x"
     :meta "The invariant is the algebraic essence of the verb; a single counter-example would falsify it and prove the implementation broken. See book-of-math ch15-multivariable-linear-algebra.")
    (:claim "Along a coordinate axis, the partial reduces to the ordinary derivative of the single-variable slice."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* 3 (cadr p)))))\n(define slice (lambda (x) (f (list x 5))))\n(< (abs (- (calc/partial f 0 (list 2 5)) (calc/derivative slice 2))) 0.05)" :tags (math program)))
     :invariant "∂f/∂x_i(a) = d/dt f(a + t e_i)|_{t=0}"
     :meta "The partial IS the 1-D derivative of the restriction that freezes every other coordinate; this identity is the bridge from calc/derivative to calc/partial."
     )
    (:claim "The partial is linear in the function: ∂(af + bg)/∂x = a ∂f/∂x + b ∂g/∂x."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (* (car p) (car p))))\n(define g (lambda (p) (* (car p) (cadr p))))\n(define h (lambda (p) (+ (* 2 (f p)) (* 3 (g p)))))\n(< (abs (- (calc/partial h 0 (list 1 1)) (+ (* 2 (calc/partial f 0 (list 1 1))) (* 3 (calc/partial g 0 (list 1 1)))))) 0.1)" :tags (cs program)))
     :invariant "∂(af + bg)/∂x = a ∂f/∂x + b ∂g/∂x"
     :meta "Linearity in the operand lets a fuzzer split any partial of a sum into partials of its terms — the algebraic law every autodiff engine relies on for correctness."
     )
    (:eng "The runnable claim doubles as a CI smoke test — if the invariant ever prints #f, the verb has drifted."
     :math "The invariant is the defining property in the calc-nd category."
     :cs "Property-based test: the program above is a runnable falsifier candidate for a QuickCheck-style fuzzer."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   
    (:base "calc/partial — see :summary."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(list (calc/partial f 0 (list 1 1)) (calc/partial f 1 (list 1 1)) (calc/gradient f (list 1 1)))" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "gradient-component"
     :meta "The emergent gradient-component belongs to the family of composed behaviours where one verb's output feeds the next verb's input, and the pair carries meaning neither verb has alone. Partial + assemble = gradient; the building block of every autodiff engine.")
    (:base "calc/partial nested to assemble a Hessian entry."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p) (cadr p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(define fx (lambda (q) (calc/partial f 0 q)))\n(list 'fxx (calc/partial fx 0 (list 1 1)) 'fxy (calc/partial fx 1 (list 1 1)))" :tags (math composition)))
     :emergent "hessian-entry"
     :meta "Applying calc/partial twice produces a second-order partial; tiling this over all index pairs builds the Hessian matrix. The composition is the scalar engine under calc/hessian."
     )
    (:base "calc/partial over every axis to build a directional derivative via the gradient dot product."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(define grad (list (calc/partial f 0 (list 1 2)) (calc/partial f 1 (list 1 2))))\n(define dir (list 0.6 0.8))\n(+ (* (car grad) (car dir)) (* (cadr grad) (cadr dir)))" :tags (cs composition)))
     :emergent "directional-derivative"
     :meta "Assemble the partials into ∇f, then dot with a unit direction to get the rate of change along any ray — this is calc/directional-derivative reconstructed from its atoms."
     )
    (:eng "The composition is short enough to inline in a callsite yet meaningful enough to name and reuse."
     :math "Under composition, the calc-nd operations close into the gradient-component pattern."
     :cs "The emergent gradient-component is a first-class named pattern; recognize it once and it reappears everywhere."))
)

(verb
  :name "calc/radius-of-convergence" :std "Motoi"
  :library "calc"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(calc/radius-of-convergence coeffs) -> (radius . interval) | (escalate 'fuel-budget)"
  :summary "Computes the radius and interval of convergence for a power series Σc(n)xⁿ using the ratio test."
  :explanation "A power series Σc(n)xⁿ converges for |x| < R, where R is the radius of convergence. calc/radius-of-convergence takes a list of coefficients [c(0), c(1), c(2), ...] and applies the ratio test (lim |c(n+1)/c(n)|) to determine R. It returns a pair (radius . interval), where interval is a list giving the convergence domain. This is useful for understanding the domain where a power series approximation is valid."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(define exp-coeffs (list 1 1 (/ 1 2) (/ 1 6) (/ 1 24) (/ 1 120)))\n(define radius-result (calc/radius-of-convergence exp-coeffs))\n(display radius-result)" :note "Radius of convergence for e^x series (coefficients 1/n!); should be infinite")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define geo-coeffs (list 1 1 1 1 1 1))\n(let ((roc (calc/radius-of-convergence geo-coeffs)))\n  (if (pair? roc)\n    (display (list \"Radius:\" (car roc) \"Interval:\" (cdr roc)))\n    (escalate 'convergence-failed geo-coeffs)))" :note "Geometric series 1 + x + x² + ... has radius 1; check the pair structure")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (analyze-series coeffs name)\n  (let ((roc (calc/radius-of-convergence coeffs)))\n    (cond ((eq? roc 'diverged) (display (list name \"series diverges everywhere\")))\n          ((pair? roc)\n            (let ((radius (car roc))\n                  (interval (cdr roc)))\n              (display (list name \"converges for x in\" interval))\n              (if (> radius 10) (display \"(very large radius; nearly entire domain)\"))\n              interval))\n          (else (escalate 'fuel-budget name)))))\n(analyze-series '(1 2 6 24 120) 'factorial-based)" :note "Robust series analysis; handle different outcomes (convergent, divergent, fuel-exhausted)")
  )
  :caveats ("Coefficients list must contain at least 2 elements; empty or single-element lists may escalate." "Ratio test assumes non-zero coefficients; exact zeros can cause division-by-zero or false results." "The test is asymptotic; for finite coefficient lists, results are approximations." "Very large coefficient sequences can exhaust the fuel budget before reaching a stable ratio; escalate 'fuel-budget in that case." "Result is deterministic within the device but may differ across platforms due to floating-point arithmetic.")
  :drawbacks ("Does not determine convergence behavior AT the boundary (|x| = R); Dirichlet/Abel tests required for those points." "Assumes the series is a power series in a single variable; multivariable or non-power series will give meaningless results." "No symbolic simplification; repeated calls for the same series are wasteful." "Ratio test is not always fastest; Cauchy root test might converge faster for some series but is not used here.")
  :usecases ("Verify that a Taylor series approximation is valid over a desired domain." "Determine the region of validity for a power-series solution to a differential equation." "Analyze the behavior of generating functions in combinatorics." "Validate numerical approximations before deployment.")
  :related ("calc/surface-integral" "calc/divergence" "calc/curl" "num/nintegrate-2d")
  :learn (:concept "The radius of convergence is a fundamental property of power series; it determines the domain where the series is a valid approximation of its limit function." :prerequisites ("power series / Taylor series" "ratio test (calculus)") :progression "After computing radius of convergence, use the series for numerical approximation within that radius, or explore other series tests (root test, Dirichlet) for behavior at the boundary.")
  :row-2-audit (
   
    (:problem "A power-series computation needs to know how far around the base point it converges; operating outside R gives nonsense."
     :programs (
       (:code "(calc/radius-of-convergence (lambda (n) (/ 1.0 (factorial n))))" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "Coefficients 1/n! give R = ∞ (exp series); operator sees a very large value."
     :meta "series-converges? tests one specific series; radius-of-convergence gives the whole disk of convergence at once.")
    (:problem "A geometric-series approximation needs its exact convergence radius so the operator can refuse inputs with |x| ≥ 1."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define geo (list 1 1 1 1 1 1 1 1))\n(let ((roc (calc/radius-of-convergence geo))) (< (abs (- (car roc) 1)) 0.2))" :tags (math program)))
     :explanation "The coefficients all-ones give R = 1 for 1 + x + x² + …; the operator reads the radius and gates x to |x| < 1."
     :meta "The radius is the exact boundary of the trust region; outside it the partial sums diverge no matter how many terms are added."
     )
    (:problem "A fuel-bounded analysis must handle the case where the ratio has not stabilised, so the operator checks for the escalation tag instead of a pair."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define coeffs (list 1 2 6 24 120))\n(let ((roc (calc/radius-of-convergence coeffs))) (or (pair? roc) (eq? roc 'diverged)))" :tags (cs program)))
     :explanation "Factorial-like growth drives R toward 0; the operator branches on whether it got a (radius . interval) pair or a divergence/escalation signal."
     :meta "The verb can escalate 'fuel-budget when the ratio never settles; the audit's contract is to inspect the return shape, not assume a number."
     )
    (:eng "The operator gets one line per call; failure surfaces the exact input that broke the expectation."
     :math "Category: series; the verb operates within its declared algebraic type."
     :cs "Complexity as declared in the signature; deterministic and testable."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "'domain of validity of a series expansion'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "calc native" :code "(calc/radius-of-convergence (lambda (n) (/ 1.0 (factorial n))))")
       (:domain "trust-region size around a Taylor approximation" :code "(calc/radius-of-convergence (lambda (n) (/ 1.0 (factorial n))))")
       (:domain "R = 1/limsup |a_n|^{1/n}" :code "(calc/radius-of-convergence (lambda (n) (/ 1.0 (factorial n))))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1 is calc/radius-of-convergence in its native library. Rung 2 shows the same ''domain of validity of a series expansion'' shape in a metaphor domain. Rung 3 lifts the shape into a math/logical setting where the transfer is formal. Recognizing the same shape in all three lets the reader carry the verb's intuition into problems Motoi does not directly cover."
     :meta "The shape transfers because ''domain of validity of a series expansion'' is substrate-independent — the same relationship holds whenever the underlying category admits the required structure.")
    (:eng "Engineers who have met the shape elsewhere already know when to reach for calc/radius-of-convergence."
     :math "The shape is captured by a small equation set; each rung is a model of that equation set."
     :cs "Once the shape is named, the same algorithm skeleton (traverse, close, compare) applies at each rung."))
  :row-4-proof (
   
    (:claim "Inside |x-c| < R the series converges absolutely; outside it diverges."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define geo (lambda (n) 1.0))\n(define R (calc/radius-of-convergence geo))\n(< 0.5 R)" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "R = 1/limsup |a_n|^{1/n}"
     :meta "The invariant is the algebraic essence of the verb; a single counter-example would falsify it and prove the implementation broken. See book-of-math ch-advanced-03 (Sequences, Series, Recurrence).")
    (:claim "The exponential series 1/n! has infinite radius of convergence."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define exp-c (list 1 1 0.5 (/ 1.0 6) (/ 1.0 24) (/ 1.0 120) (/ 1.0 720)))\n(> (car (calc/radius-of-convergence exp-c)) 5)" :tags (math program)))
     :invariant "a_n = 1/n! ⇒ R = ∞"
     :meta "Factorial decay outpaces every geometric rate, so the ratio |a_{n+1}/a_n| = 1/(n+1) → 0 and R = 1/0 = ∞. This is why eˣ is entire — its Taylor series converges everywhere."
     )
    (:claim "Multiplying every coefficient by a constant leaves the radius of convergence unchanged."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define base (list 1 1 1 1 1 1))\n(define scaled (list 5 5 5 5 5 5))\n(< (abs (- (car (calc/radius-of-convergence base)) (car (calc/radius-of-convergence scaled)))) 0.2)" :tags (cs program)))
     :invariant "R(c·a_n) = R(a_n)  for constant c ≠ 0"
     :meta "A constant factor cancels in the ratio |a_{n+1}/a_n|, so R depends only on the asymptotic growth of the coefficients, not their overall scale."
     )
    (:eng "The runnable claim doubles as a CI smoke test — if the invariant ever prints #f, the verb has drifted."
     :math "The invariant is the defining property in the series category."
     :cs "Property-based test: the program above is a runnable falsifier candidate for a QuickCheck-style fuzzer."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   
    (:base "calc/radius-of-convergence — see :summary."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define coeffs (list (lambda (n) (/ 1.0 (factorial n))) (lambda (n) 1.0)))\n(map calc/radius-of-convergence coeffs)" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "convergence-disk"
     :meta "The emergent convergence-disk belongs to the family of composed behaviours where one verb's output feeds the next verb's input, and the pair carries meaning neither verb has alone. Radius of convergence + Taylor series is the standard function-approximation pattern.")
    (:base "calc/radius-of-convergence gating a Taylor evaluation to its safe domain."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define geo (list 1 1 1 1 1 1))\n(define R (car (calc/radius-of-convergence geo)))\n(define safe-eval (lambda (x) (if (< (abs x) R) (/ 1.0 (- 1 x)) 'outside-radius)))\n(list (safe-eval 0.5) (safe-eval 2))" :tags (math composition)))
     :emergent "guarded-series-sum"
     :meta "The radius becomes a runtime guard: inside R the closed form 1/(1−x) is trustworthy, outside it the series diverges and the composition refuses. Trust-region enforcement in one expression."
     )
    (:base "calc/radius-of-convergence sweeping a family of series to rank their approximation ranges."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define families (list (list 1 1 0.5 (/ 1.0 6)) (list 1 1 1 1) (list 1 2 4 8)))\n(map (lambda (c) (car (calc/radius-of-convergence c))) families)" :tags (cs composition)))
     :emergent "radius-spectrum"
     :meta "Mapping the verb over several coefficient lists yields a spectrum of convergence radii (∞, 1, 1/2), letting a caller pick the series with the widest safe domain for a given target point."
     )
    (:eng "The composition is short enough to inline in a callsite yet meaningful enough to name and reuse."
     :math "Under composition, the series operations close into the convergence-disk pattern."
     :cs "The emergent convergence-disk is a first-class named pattern; recognize it once and it reappears everywhere."))
)

(verb
  :name "calc/riemann-sum" :std "Motoi"
  :library "calc"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(calc/riemann-sum f a b kind n) -> number"
  :summary "Approximates the definite integral ∫ₐᵇ f(x)dx using Riemann sums with rectangles."
  :explanation "A Riemann sum estimates the area under a curve by dividing the interval [a,b] into n rectangles and summing their areas. The kind parameter controls rectangle height: 'left uses left endpoints, 'right uses right endpoints, 'mid uses midpoints. Left and right sums bracket the true integral (for monotone functions); midpoint is more accurate. This verb is foundational for understanding integration and is useful when symbolically computing the integral is intractable."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(calc/riemann-sum (lambda (x) (* x x)) 0 1 'mid 100)" :note "∫₀¹ x² dx = 1/3 ≈ 0.333; midpoint with 100 subintervals gives good approximation")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define f (lambda (x) (math/exp (- (* x x)))))\n(calc/riemann-sum f -2 2 'mid 500)" :note "Gaussian bell curve; ∫₋₂² e^(−x²) dx ≈ 1.76; use more subintervals for better accuracy")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (convergence-test f a b kind)\n  (map (lambda (n) (list 'n n 'sum (calc/riemann-sum f a b kind n)))\n       '(10 50 100 500 1000)))\n(convergence-test (lambda (x) (sin x)) 0 3.14159 'mid)" :note "Tests how Riemann sum converges as n increases; shows that midpoint converges faster than left/right")
  )
  :caveats ("Accuracy depends on n: more rectangles = better approximation but slower computation" "Left and right sums are biased; 'mid is more accurate but not always best for oscillating functions" "Does not detect or handle discontinuities in f on [a,b]" "Parameter order: f, a (lower limit), b (upper limit), kind (symbol), n (integer subintervals)")
  :drawbacks ("O(1/n) convergence: need n~1000s for 3–4 decimal places (slow compared to Simpson's rule)" "Does not work well for f with sharp peaks or discontinuities" "Left/right sums give different results; need to use both to bracket the true integral" "For smooth functions, calc/integral (Simpson) is significantly faster")
  :usecases ("Teaching integration: visualizing how area is approximated by rectangles" "Estimating probability (area under density curve) when symbolic form is unknown" "Computing work or energy in physics (∫ F·dx)" "Baseline accuracy check when implementing advanced quadrature")
  :related ("calc/integral" "calc/average-value" "calc/arc-length" "calc/volume-revolution")
  :learn (:concept "Riemann sums as foundation of the Riemann integral; convergence via refinement" :prerequisites () :progression "Master Riemann sums with 'mid kind, then move to calc/integral for faster Simpson's rule")
  :row-2-audit (
   
    (:problem "A pedagogy or budget check needs an explicit Riemann sum with a chosen mesh, so the operator can compare left/right/midpoint approximations."
     :programs (
       (:code "(calc/riemann-sum (lambda (x) (* x x)) 0 1 100)" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "100 rectangles for ∫₀¹ x² dx ≈ 0.33; operator sees a value near 0.333."
     :meta "calc/integral hides the mesh; calc/riemann-sum exposes it. Use riemann-sum when the mesh matters (education, controlled experiment).")
    (:problem "A bracketing check needs left and right sums for a monotone function, so the operator can trap the true integral between them."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (* x x)))\n(let ((lo (calc/riemann-sum f 0 1 'left 200)) (hi (calc/riemann-sum f 0 1 'right 200)))\n  (and (< lo (/ 1.0 3)) (< (/ 1.0 3) hi)))" :tags (math program)))
     :explanation "For the increasing x², the left sum under-estimates and the right sum over-estimates; the true value 1/3 sits between them, so the check returns #t."
     :meta "The choice of endpoint (left/right/mid) is the mesh policy the operator controls; left and right sums give a rigorous bracket that midpoint alone does not."
     )
    (:problem "A convergence-rate demonstration needs the same integral at growing mesh counts so the operator can watch the error shrink."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (sin x)))\n(map (lambda (n) (calc/riemann-sum f 0 3.14159 'mid n)) (list 10 100 1000))" :tags (cs program)))
     :explanation "∫₀^π sin = 2; the three midpoint sums march toward 2 as n grows, exposing the O(1/n²) midpoint convergence the operator wants to see."
     :meta "Exposing the mesh count n as a knob is the whole point of riemann-sum over the adaptive calc/integral — the operator studies the approximation, not just its answer."
     )
    (:eng "The operator gets one line per call; failure surfaces the exact input that broke the expectation."
     :math "Category: calc-1d; the verb operates within its declared algebraic type."
     :cs "Complexity as declared in the signature; deterministic and testable."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "'discrete approximation of a continuous accumulation'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "calc native" :code "(calc/riemann-sum (lambda (x) (* x x)) 0 1 100)")
       (:domain "audio RMS from samples" :code "(calc/riemann-sum (lambda (x) (* x x)) 0 1 100)")
       (:domain "Riemann sum" :code "(calc/riemann-sum (lambda (x) (* x x)) 0 1 100)"))
     :explanation "Rung 1 is calc/riemann-sum in its native library. Rung 2 shows the same ''discrete approximation of a continuous accumulation'' shape in a metaphor domain. Rung 3 lifts the shape into a math/logical setting where the transfer is formal. Recognizing the same shape in all three lets the reader carry the verb's intuition into problems Motoi does not directly cover."
     :meta "The shape transfers because ''discrete approximation of a continuous accumulation'' is substrate-independent — the same relationship holds whenever the underlying category admits the required structure.")
    (:eng "Engineers who have met the shape elsewhere already know when to reach for calc/riemann-sum."
     :math "The shape is captured by a small equation set; each rung is a model of that equation set."
     :cs "Once the shape is named, the same algorithm skeleton (traverse, close, compare) applies at each rung."))
  :row-4-proof (
   
    (:claim "As mesh size → 0, the Riemann sum → the Riemann integral (for continuous f)."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (* x x)))\n(< (abs (- (calc/riemann-sum f 0 1 1000) (calc/integral f 0 1))) 0.01)" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "lim_{n→∞} (1/n) Σ_i f(a + iΔx)Δx = ∫_a^b f"
     :meta "The invariant is the algebraic essence of the verb; a single counter-example would falsify it and prove the implementation broken. See book-of-math ch13-calculus-1 and ch14-calculus-2.")
    (:claim "The Riemann sum is additive over adjacent subintervals: sum over [a,c] = sum over [a,b] + sum over [b,c] at matching mesh width."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (* x x)))\n(< (abs (- (calc/riemann-sum f 0 2 'mid 200) (+ (calc/riemann-sum f 0 1 'mid 100) (calc/riemann-sum f 1 2 'mid 100)))) 0.01)" :tags (math program)))
     :invariant "S_[a,c] = S_[a,b] + S_[b,c]  (matched Δx)"
     :meta "Additivity of the sum mirrors additivity of the integral it approximates; splitting the domain and recombining must leave the total area unchanged."
     )
    (:claim "For a monotone increasing f, the midpoint sum lies between the left and right sums."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (math/exp x)))\n(let ((l (calc/riemann-sum f 0 1 'left 100)) (m (calc/riemann-sum f 0 1 'mid 100)) (r (calc/riemann-sum f 0 1 'right 100)))\n  (and (< l m) (< m r)))" :tags (cs program)))
     :invariant "f increasing ⇒ S_left ≤ S_mid ≤ S_right"
     :meta "The ordering of the three rules is a structural property of monotone functions; a fuzzer that flips it would have found either a non-monotone input or an implementation bug."
     )
    (:eng "The runnable claim doubles as a CI smoke test — if the invariant ever prints #f, the verb has drifted."
     :math "The invariant is the defining property in the calc-1d category."
     :cs "Property-based test: the program above is a runnable falsifier candidate for a QuickCheck-style fuzzer."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   
    (:base "calc/riemann-sum — see :summary."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (* x x)))\n(map (lambda (n) (calc/riemann-sum f 0 1 n)) (list 10 100 1000))" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "mesh-refinement-sequence"
     :meta "The emergent mesh-refinement-sequence belongs to the family of composed behaviours where one verb's output feeds the next verb's input, and the pair carries meaning neither verb has alone. Mesh refinement → convergent sum = the classical numerical integration pattern.")
    (:base "calc/riemann-sum differenced against calc/integral to estimate discretisation error."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (math/exp (- (* x x))))) \n(define exact (calc/integral f -2 2))\n(map (lambda (n) (abs (- (calc/riemann-sum f -2 2 'mid n) exact))) (list 10 100 1000))" :tags (math composition)))
     :emergent "error-curve"
     :meta "Subtracting the reference (Simpson) value from each Riemann sum traces the error as a function of mesh count — the empirical convergence-order plot used to validate a quadrature."
     )
    (:base "calc/riemann-sum applied to a probability density to build an empirical CDF."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define pdf (lambda (x) (* 2 x)))\n(define cdf (lambda (t) (calc/riemann-sum pdf 0 t 'mid 200)))\n(map cdf (list 0.25 0.5 1.0))" :tags (cs composition)))
     :emergent "empirical-cdf"
     :meta "Sweeping the upper limit turns the Riemann sum into a running cumulative probability; the mesh-visible version of the CDF pattern, useful when the density is only known pointwise."
     )
    (:eng "The composition is short enough to inline in a callsite yet meaningful enough to name and reuse."
     :math "Under composition, the calc-1d operations close into the mesh-refinement-sequence pattern."
     :cs "The emergent mesh-refinement-sequence is a first-class named pattern; recognize it once and it reappears everywhere."))
)

(verb
  :name "calc/second-derivative" :std "Motoi"
  :library "calc"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(calc/second-derivative f x) -> number"
  :summary "Computes the second derivative f''(x) using central differences, measuring curvature."
  :explanation "The second derivative describes how fast the slope is changing: how curved the graph is at a point. Positive f''(x) means the curve is concave up (bending upward); negative means concave down. The second derivative is used to classify critical points (local max/min via second derivative test), estimate errors in Taylor approximations, and solve differential equations. Numerically computed via central-difference formula on the first-derivative approximation."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(calc/second-derivative (lambda (x) (expt x 3)) 1)\n(calc/second-derivative (lambda (x) (expt x 3)) -1)" :note "f(x) = x³ has f''(x) = 6x; at x=1 result ≈ 6, at x=−1 result ≈ −6")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define (classify-critical-point f x0)\n  (let ((fpp (calc/second-derivative f x0)))\n    (cond ((> fpp 0.01) 'local-minimum)\n          ((< fpp -0.01) 'local-maximum)\n          (else 'inconclusive))))\n(classify-critical-point (lambda (x) (- (* x x) (* 4 x))) 2)" :note "f(x) = x² − 4x; critical point at x=2; f''(2) = 2 > 0 so it's a local minimum")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (filter pred xs)\n  (let loop ((xs xs) (acc '()))\n    (cond ((null? xs) (reverse acc))\n          ((pred (car xs)) (loop (cdr xs) (cons (car xs) acc)))\n          (else (loop (cdr xs) acc)))))\n(define (find-inflection-points f a b n)\n  (define h (/ (- b a) n))\n  (define pts (map (lambda (k) (+ a (* k h))) (range 0 n)))\n  (filter (lambda (x) (if (< x (- b h)) \n                          (let ((fpp-left (calc/second-derivative f (- x h)))\n                                (fpp-right (calc/second-derivative f (+ x h))))\n                            (< (* fpp-left fpp-right) 0))\n                          #f))\n          pts))\n(find-inflection-points (lambda (x) (expt x 5)) -1 1 20)" :note "Finds inflection points where f''(x) changes sign; x⁵ has one at x=0")
  )
  :caveats ("Numeric approximation; accuracy depends on h and function smoothness" "May be noisy on nearly-linear functions (f''(x) ≈ 0 across range)" "If f'(x) is discontinuous, f''(x) will be very noisy or unreliable" "Central-difference formula requires evaluating f at three points; does not reuse derivative calculations")
  :drawbacks ("More expensive than first derivative (3 function evaluations vs. implicit reuse)" "Amplifies numerical noise: if f is given by finite-precision data, f'' is noisier than f'" "Does not detect or handle jump discontinuities in f' (where f'' is undefined)" "For piecewise smooth functions, may give misleading results near kinks")
  :usecases ("Second derivative test: classifying critical points as local max, min, or saddle" "Detecting concavity changes (inflection points)" "Estimating truncation error in Taylor approximations (error ≈ f''(ξ)·h³/6)" "Studying oscillation damping in mechanics (f'' = acceleration in x = position)")
  :related ("calc/derivative" "calc/extrema-1d" "calc/hessian" "calc/taylor")
  :learn (:concept "Second derivative as curvature; concavity and inflection points" :prerequisites ("calc/derivative") :progression "Use calc/second-derivative for univariate optimization, then calc/hessian for multivariate")
  :row-2-audit (
   
    (:problem "An inflection-point analysis or curvature computation needs f''(x); the operator wants a direct call rather than differentiating twice manually."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (* x x x)))\n(calc/second-derivative f 1)" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "f'' of x³ at 1 is 6; operator sees ≈ 6."
     :meta "derivative gives f'; second-derivative gives f''. Wrapping (define (fp x) (calc/derivative f x)) and taking (calc/derivative fp x) works too but is noisier.")
    (:problem "A concavity read-out at a point needs the sign of f'', so the operator can label the graph as bending up or down there."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (* x x)))\n(if (> (calc/second-derivative f 3) 0) 'concave-up 'concave-down)" :tags (math program)))
     :explanation "f''(x) = 2 > 0 everywhere for x², so the operator reads 'concave-up — the parabola opens upward."
     :meta "The sign of the second derivative is the concavity classifier; magnitude gives the curvature strength. The audit turns the number into a shape verdict."
     )
    (:problem "A Taylor-error budget needs the size of f'' on an interval to bound the remainder term of a linear approximation."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (math/exp x)))\n(define bound (calc/second-derivative f 1))\n(< (abs (- bound (math/exp 1))) 0.05)" :tags (cs program)))
     :explanation "For eˣ, f''(1) = e ≈ 2.718; this magnitude feeds the Lagrange remainder |R₁| ≤ M·h²/2 that bounds the tangent-line error."
     :meta "The second derivative controls how fast a linear approximation degrades; the operator reads M = max|f''| to size a safe step h."
     )
    (:eng "The operator gets one line per call; failure surfaces the exact input that broke the expectation."
     :math "Category: calc-1d; the verb operates within its declared algebraic type."
     :cs "Complexity as declared in the signature; deterministic and testable."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "'second-order rate of change'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "calc native" :code "(define f (lambda (x) (* x x x)))")
       (:domain "acceleration from position samples" :code "(define f (lambda (x) (* x x)))\n(if (> (calc/second-derivative f 3) 0) 'concave-up 'concave-down)")
       (:domain "second derivative f\\'\\'(x)" :code "(define f (lambda (x) (math/exp x)))\n(define bound (calc/second-derivative f 1))\n(< (abs (- bound (math/exp 1))) 0.05)"))
     :explanation "Rung 1 is calc/second-derivative in its native library. Rung 2 shows the same ''second-order rate of change'' shape in a metaphor domain. Rung 3 lifts the shape into a math/logical setting where the transfer is formal. Recognizing the same shape in all three lets the reader carry the verb's intuition into problems Motoi does not directly cover."
     :meta "The shape transfers because ''second-order rate of change'' is substrate-independent — the same relationship holds whenever the underlying category admits the required structure.")
    (:eng "Engineers who have met the shape elsewhere already know when to reach for calc/second-derivative."
     :math "The shape is captured by a small equation set; each rung is a model of that equation set."
     :cs "Once the shape is named, the same algorithm skeleton (traverse, close, compare) applies at each rung."))
  :row-4-proof (
   
    (:claim "At a local max, f'' ≤ 0; at a local min, f'' ≥ 0 (second-derivative test)."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (* x x)))\n(> (calc/second-derivative f 0) 0)" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "local min at c ⇒ f''(c) ≥ 0  ∧  local max at c ⇒ f''(c) ≤ 0"
     :meta "The invariant is the algebraic essence of the verb; a single counter-example would falsify it and prove the implementation broken. See book-of-math ch13-calculus-1 and ch14-calculus-2.")
    (:claim "The second derivative equals the derivative of the derivative: f''(x) = (f')'(x)."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (* x x x)))\n(define (fp x) (calc/derivative f x))\n(< (abs (- (calc/second-derivative f 2) (calc/derivative fp 2))) 0.2)" :tags (math program)))
     :invariant "f''(x) = d/dx (f'(x))"
     :meta "The direct central-difference second derivative must agree with iterating calc/derivative twice; the direct form is just less noisy. This ties calc/second-derivative to calc/derivative."
     )
    (:claim "The second derivative of a straight line is zero everywhere."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define line (lambda (x) (+ (* 4 x) 7)))\n(< (abs (calc/second-derivative line 5)) 0.01)" :tags (cs program)))
     :invariant "f linear ⇒ f''(x) = 0 ∀ x"
     :meta "Curvature is exactly what distinguishes a line from a curve; a nonzero second derivative on an affine function would signal numerical noise or a broken stencil."
     )
    (:eng "The runnable claim doubles as a CI smoke test — if the invariant ever prints #f, the verb has drifted."
     :math "The invariant is the defining property in the calc-1d category."
     :cs "Property-based test: the program above is a runnable falsifier candidate for a QuickCheck-style fuzzer."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   
    (:base "calc/second-derivative — see :summary."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (sin x)))\n(map (lambda (x) (calc/second-derivative f x)) (list 0 (/ 3.14159 2) 3.14159))" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "convexity-test"
     :meta "The emergent convexity-test belongs to the family of composed behaviours where one verb's output feeds the next verb's input, and the pair carries meaning neither verb has alone. Second-derivative test = classical convexity analysis; extended in book-of-ml ch-06 (gradient descent).")
    (:base "calc/second-derivative paired with calc/newton on f' to classify a stationary point."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (+ (* x x x x) (- (* 2 x x)))))\n(define (fp x) (calc/derivative f x))\n(define crit (calc/newton fp 1.5))\n(if (> (calc/second-derivative f crit) 0) 'minimum 'maximum)" :tags (math composition)))
     :emergent "second-derivative-test"
     :meta "Root-of-derivative finds the stationary point; the sign of f'' there labels it min or max. This is the full classical extremum classifier, the univariate core of calc/extrema-1d."
     )
    (:base "calc/second-derivative sampled to detect an inflection by sign change."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (* x x x)))\n(list (calc/second-derivative f -1) (calc/second-derivative f 1))" :tags (cs composition)))
     :emergent "inflection-detector"
     :meta "f'' of x³ flips from −6 to +6 across x=0; a sign change in the second derivative pins an inflection point, where concavity reverses. Watching two samples straddle zero is the detector."
     )
    (:eng "The composition is short enough to inline in a callsite yet meaningful enough to name and reuse."
     :math "Under composition, the calc-1d operations close into the convexity-test pattern."
     :cs "The emergent convexity-test is a first-class named pattern; recognize it once and it reappears everywhere."))
)

(verb
  :name "calc/series-converges?" :std "Motoi"
  :library "calc"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(calc/series-converges? coeff-fn [opts]) -> 'converges | 'diverges | 'inconclusive"
  :summary "Apply the ratio test to a series Σa(n) and return convergence verdict."
  :explanation "The ratio test is a standard tool in analysis: if lim |a(n+1)/a(n)| = L, then the series converges absolutely if L < 1, diverges if L > 1, and is inconclusive if L = 1. This verb computes that limit numerically via finite differences and returns one of three symbolic outcomes. Use it to classify infinite series before attempting to work with them. The verdict is deterministic within the precision of the device."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(calc/series-converges? (lambda (n) (/ 1 (+ n 1))))" :note "Harmonic series 1/n diverges; ratio test → 'inconclusive (limit ratio = 1, edge case)")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(calc/series-converges? (lambda (n) (/ 1 (* 2 n))))" :note "Geometric-like series with ratio 1/2 < 1 → 'converges")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (factorial n) (if (<= n 1) 1 (* n (factorial (- n 1)))))\n(let ((test-series (lambda (n) (/ (factorial n) (expt 2 n)))))\n  (cond ((equal? (calc/series-converges? test-series) 'converges)\n         (display \"safe to sum\"))\n        (else (display \"risky or inconclusive\"))))" :note "Factorial grows faster than exponential; guards downstream computation")
  )
  :caveats ("The ratio test is inconclusive when lim(ratio) = 1; the verb returns 'inconclusive in that case, not a boolean" "Requires a coefficient function, not a pre-computed list; the function is called repeatedly to build ratios" "Numerical precision limits detection at extremes; very slow convergence or divergence may be misclassified" "The verb does not sum the series; it only predicts convergence. Use calc/line-integral, calc/surface-integral, or num/nintegrate-* for actual computation")
  :drawbacks ("Ratio test fails silently on edge cases (L = 1); other tests (root test, integral test) may succeed where ratio test is inconclusive" "Expensive if the coefficient function is costly to evaluate" "Limited to scalar series; vector/matrix series require different machinery")
  :usecases ("Before summing a power series, check convergence to avoid infinite loops" "Validate that a Fourier series representation is mathematically sound" "Classify solutions to differential equations that are given as series")
  :related ("calc/radius-of-convergence" "calc/line-integral" "calc/surface-integral" "num/nintegrate-2d" "num/nintegrate-3d")
  :learn (:concept "Ratio test as a tool for series classification; how symbolic outcomes encode mathematical uncertainty" :prerequisites ("(core)/lambda") :progression "Move to calc/radius-of-convergence to analyze power series; then to num/nintegrate-* to compute definite integrals")
  :row-2-audit (
   
    (:problem "A summation over an infinite series needs a pre-check: does the series converge at all, or is the computation meaningless?"
     :programs (
       (:code "(list (calc/series-converges? (lambda (n) (/ 1.0 (* n n))))\n      (calc/series-converges? (lambda (n) (/ 1.0 n))))" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "Σ 1/n² converges (π²/6); Σ 1/n diverges (harmonic); operator sees (#t #f)."
     :meta "radius-of-convergence is for power series; series-converges? is for a single numeric series. Different domains.")
    (:problem "A factorial-vs-exponential race needs a verdict before summing, since factorial growth overwhelms any fixed base and the sum blows up."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define (factorial n) (if (<= n 1) 1 (* n (factorial (- n 1)))))\n(not (eq? (calc/series-converges? (lambda (n) (/ (factorial n) (expt 2 n)))) 'converges))" :tags (math program)))
     :explanation "The ratio |a_{n+1}/a_n| = (n+1)/2 → ∞ > 1, so the ratio test verdict is 'diverges; the operator refuses to sum."
     :meta "The ratio test is decisive here (L = ∞); the operator's audit is a guard that prevents an infinite accumulation loop downstream."
     )
    (:problem "A boundary series with ratio limit exactly 1 needs its 'inconclusive verdict surfaced, so the operator knows to reach for a finer test."
     :programs (
       (:code "(eq? (calc/series-converges? (lambda (n) (/ 1.0 (+ n 1)))) 'inconclusive)" :tags (cs program)))
     :explanation "The harmonic series has ratio limit 1, so the ratio test returns 'inconclusive rather than a boolean; the operator must escalate to the integral or comparison test."
     :meta "The three-valued output is the honest part of the contract: L = 1 is genuinely undecided by the ratio test, and the verb reports that instead of guessing."
     )
    (:eng "The operator gets one line per call; failure surfaces the exact input that broke the expectation."
     :math "Category: series; the verb operates within its declared algebraic type."
     :cs "Complexity as declared in the signature; deterministic and testable."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "'convergence gate for infinite sums'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "calc native" :code "(list (calc/series-converges? (lambda (n) (/ 1.0 (* n n))))\n      (calc/series-converges? (lambda (n) (/ 1.0 n))))")
       (:domain "idle-loop bounded-effect check" :code "(define diverges (lambda (n) 1.0))\n(not (calc/series-converges? diverges))")
       (:domain "convergence of Σ a_n" :code "(eq? (calc/series-converges? (lambda (n) (expt 0.5 n))) 'converges)"))
     :explanation "Rung 1 is calc/series-converges? in its native library. Rung 2 shows the same ''convergence gate for infinite sums'' shape in a metaphor domain. Rung 3 lifts the shape into a math/logical setting where the transfer is formal. Recognizing the same shape in all three lets the reader carry the verb's intuition into problems Motoi does not directly cover."
     :meta "The shape transfers because ''convergence gate for infinite sums'' is substrate-independent — the same relationship holds whenever the underlying category admits the required structure.")
    (:eng "Engineers who have met the shape elsewhere already know when to reach for calc/series-converges?."
     :math "The shape is captured by a small equation set; each rung is a model of that equation set."
     :cs "Once the shape is named, the same algorithm skeleton (traverse, close, compare) applies at each rung."))
  :row-4-proof (
   
    (:claim "If Σ a_n converges then a_n → 0 (necessary condition)."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define diverges (lambda (n) 1.0))\n(not (calc/series-converges? diverges))" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "Σ a_n converges ⇒ lim_{n→∞} a_n = 0"
     :meta "The invariant is the algebraic essence of the verb; a single counter-example would falsify it and prove the implementation broken. See book-of-math ch-advanced-03 (Sequences, Series, Recurrence).")
    (:claim "A geometric series with common ratio |r| < 1 converges."
     :programs (
       (:code "(eq? (calc/series-converges? (lambda (n) (expt 0.5 n))) 'converges)" :tags (math program)))
     :invariant "|r| < 1 ⇒ Σ r^n converges to 1/(1−r)"
     :meta "The geometric series is the reference case for the ratio test: the ratio is exactly r, so L = |r| < 1 gives clean convergence. The archetype every ratio-test proof reduces to."
     )
    (:claim "When the ratio limit equals 1 the test is inconclusive, not a false verdict."
     :programs (
       (:code "(eq? (calc/series-converges? (lambda (n) (/ 1.0 (+ n 1)))) 'inconclusive)" :tags (cs program)))
     :invariant "lim |a_{n+1}/a_n| = 1 ⇒ verdict = 'inconclusive"
     :meta "Honesty at the boundary is a correctness property: returning 'converges or 'diverges at L=1 would be a bug, since the ratio test genuinely cannot decide there."
     )
    (:eng "The runnable claim doubles as a CI smoke test — if the invariant ever prints #f, the verb has drifted."
     :math "The invariant is the defining property in the series category."
     :cs "Property-based test: the program above is a runnable falsifier candidate for a QuickCheck-style fuzzer."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   
    (:base "calc/series-converges? — see :summary."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define seqs (list (lambda (n) (/ 1.0 (* n n))) (lambda (n) (/ 1.0 (expt 2 n))) (lambda (n) (/ 1.0 n))))\n(map calc/series-converges? seqs)" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "convergence-classifier"
     :meta "The emergent convergence-classifier belongs to the family of composed behaviours where one verb's output feeds the next verb's input, and the pair carries meaning neither verb has alone. Same shape as a program-termination check — decide before running.")
    (:base "calc/series-converges? gating a summation loop so divergent inputs are refused."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define a (lambda (n) (/ 1.0 (expt 2 n))))\n(define safe-sum (lambda (terms) (if (eq? (calc/series-converges? a) 'converges) (apply + (map a (range 0 terms))) 'refused)))\n(safe-sum 30)" :tags (math composition)))
     :emergent "guarded-summation"
     :meta "The convergence verdict becomes a precondition on the summation; only 'converges unlocks the accumulation, mirroring a termination check before running an unbounded loop."
     )
    (:base "calc/series-converges? routed with calc/radius-of-convergence for a full power-series verdict."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define (factorial n) (if (<= n 1) 1 (* n (factorial (- n 1)))))\n(define coeffs (list 1 1 0.5 (/ 1.0 6) (/ 1.0 24)))\n(define term (lambda (n) (/ 1.0 (factorial n))))\n(list 'per-series (calc/series-converges? term) 'radius (car (calc/radius-of-convergence coeffs)))" :tags (cs composition)))
     :emergent "series-diagnosis"
     :meta "series-converges? judges the numeric series at a point; radius-of-convergence reports the whole disk. Running both gives a complete diagnosis of where a power series may be trusted."
     )
    (:eng "The composition is short enough to inline in a callsite yet meaningful enough to name and reuse."
     :math "Under composition, the series operations close into the convergence-classifier pattern."
     :cs "The emergent convergence-classifier is a first-class named pattern; recognize it once and it reappears everywhere."))
)

(verb
  :name "calc/surface-integral" :std "Motoi"
  :library "calc"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(calc/surface-integral surface-fn field-fn mode [opts]) -> number | error"
  :summary "Integrate a scalar field or vector field over a parametric surface."
  :explanation "A surface integral generalizes single-variable integration to two-dimensional surfaces embedded in 3D (or lower). Two modes: 'scalar integrates a scalar function f over surface area dS; 'flux integrates a vector field F's normal component (F · n) over the surface, measuring flow. The surface is defined parametrically, and the verb uses numerical quadrature (tensor Simpson's rule) to approximate the integral. Essential in physics (flux through a membrane, work on a surface) and engineering."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(calc/surface-integral\n  (lambda (u v) (list u v (+ u v)))  ;; surface: z = u + v\n  (lambda (x y z) 1)                   ;; scalar field f = 1\n  'scalar)" :note "Integrating constant 1 over a planar surface gives its area")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(calc/surface-integral\n  (lambda (u v) (list (* (cos u) (sin v)) (* (sin u) (sin v)) (cos v)))  ;; unit sphere\n  (lambda (x y z) (+ (* x x) (* y y) (* z z)))                          ;; f = x² + y² + z²\n  'scalar)" :note "On a sphere, x² + y² + z² = 1, so the integral should be surface area 4π")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (flux-through-cylinder h r)\n  (calc/surface-integral\n    (lambda (theta z) (list (* r (cos theta)) (* r (sin theta)) z))  ;; cylinder\n    (lambda (x y z) (list x y 0))  ;; radial outward field F = (x, y, 0)\n    'flux))\n(flux-through-cylinder 2 1)" :note "Flux of outward field through cylinder wall (height 2, radius 1); integrates outward normal component; expect 4π")
  )
  :caveats ("Requires a parametric surface function (u, v) → [x, y, z], not an implicit surface z = f(x, y)" "'scalar mode integrates over dS (magnitude of normal); 'flux mode integrates F · n (oriented normal)" "The parametrization's domain (u, v ranges) must be specified in opts or defaults to [0, 1] × [0, 1]" "Quadrature may fail or return high error if the surface is singular or the field is discontinuous")
  :drawbacks ("Numerical integration cost grows with required precision; complex surfaces or fine meshes are expensive" "'flux mode requires an oriented surface; non-orientable surfaces (e.g., Möbius band) give undefined results" "No symbolic simplification; cannot exploit symmetry automatically")
  :usecases ("Compute electric flux through a conductor surface using Gauss's law" "Integrate mass distribution over a curved membrane" "Verify divergence theorem: compare surface integral of F·n against volume integral of ∇·F")
  :related ("calc/curl" "calc/divergence" "calc/line-integral" "num/nintegrate-2d" "num/nintegrate-3d")
  :learn (:concept "Surface integrals as flux and scalar measures; parametric representation of surfaces; oriented vs. unoriented" :prerequisites ("(core)/lambda" "calc/divergence" "calc/curl") :progression "After surface integrals, explore divergence theorem and Stokes' theorem by comparing surface and volume/line integrals")
  :row-2-audit (
   
    (:problem "A flux computation through a curved surface needs ∫∫_S F·dS; the operator wants a scalar output for the total flow."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define F (lambda (p) (list (car p) (cadr p) (caddr p))))\n(define x (lambda (u v) (* (cos u) (sin v))))\n(define y (lambda (u v) (* (sin u) (sin v))))\n(define z (lambda (u v) (cos v)))\n(calc/surface-integral F x y z 0 (* 2 3.14159) 0 3.14159)" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "Flux of position field through unit sphere = 4π; operator sees ≈ 12.57."
     :meta "line-integral is 1D over a curve; surface-integral is 2D over a surface. Same idea, different dimensionality.")
    (:problem "A surface-area measurement needs the scalar surface integral ∫∫ 1 dS of the constant field over a parametric patch."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define sphere (lambda (u v) (list (* (cos u) (sin v)) (* (sin u) (sin v)) (cos v))))\n(define one (lambda (x y z) 1))\n(number? (calc/surface-integral sphere one 'scalar))" :tags (math program)))
     :explanation "Integrating the scalar 1 over the unit sphere returns its area 4π ≈ 12.57; the operator reads surface area directly in 'scalar mode."
     :meta "The 'scalar mode integrates over dS (area element); the 'flux mode integrates F·n. Same verb, two integrand types, selected by the mode argument."
     )
    (:problem "A Gauss's-law electric-flux read-out needs ∮ E·n dS through a closed surface to recover the enclosed charge."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define E (lambda (x y z) (list x y z)))\n(define sphere (lambda (u v) (list (* (cos u) (sin v)) (* (sin u) (sin v)) (cos v))))\n(number? (calc/surface-integral sphere E 'flux))" :tags (cs program)))
     :explanation "The flux of the radial field E = (x,y,z) through the unit sphere is 4π, proportional to the enclosed charge by Gauss's law; the operator confirms a numeric flux."
     :meta "Flux mode integrates the oriented normal component; this is the exact quantity Gauss's law equates to enclosed charge, the workhorse of electrostatics."
     )
    (:eng "The operator gets one line per call; failure surfaces the exact input that broke the expectation."
     :math "Category: calc-nd; the verb operates within its declared algebraic type."
     :cs "Complexity as declared in the signature; deterministic and testable."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "'flux through a 2D surface'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "calc native" :code "(define F (lambda (p) (list (car p) (cadr p) (caddr p))))")
       (:domain "rendered lighting integral over a mesh" :code "(define F (lambda (p) (list (car p) (cadr p) (caddr p))))\n(define x (lambda (u v) (* (cos u) (sin v))))\n(define y (lambda (u v) (* (sin u) (sin v))))\n(define z (lambda (u v) (cos v)))\n(calc/surface-integral F x y z 0 (* 2 3.14159) 0 3.14159)")
       (:domain "∫∫_S F · dS" :code "(define sphere (lambda (u v) (list (* (cos u) (sin v)) (* (sin u) (sin v)) (cos v))))\n(define one (lambda (x y z) 1))\n(number? (calc/surface-integral sphere one 'scalar))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1 is calc/surface-integral in its native library. Rung 2 shows the same ''flux through a 2D surface'' shape in a metaphor domain. Rung 3 lifts the shape into a math/logical setting where the transfer is formal. Recognizing the same shape in all three lets the reader carry the verb's intuition into problems Motoi does not directly cover."
     :meta "The shape transfers because ''flux through a 2D surface'' is substrate-independent — the same relationship holds whenever the underlying category admits the required structure.")
    (:eng "Engineers who have met the shape elsewhere already know when to reach for calc/surface-integral."
     :math "The shape is captured by a small equation set; each rung is a model of that equation set."
     :cs "Once the shape is named, the same algorithm skeleton (traverse, close, compare) applies at each rung."))
  :row-4-proof (
   
    (:claim "For a closed surface enclosing volume V, ∫∫_S F·dS = ∫∫∫_V div F dV (Divergence theorem)."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define F (lambda (p) (list (car p) (cadr p) (caddr p))))\n(define x (lambda (u v) (* (cos u) (sin v))))\n(define y (lambda (u v) (* (sin u) (sin v))))\n(define z (lambda (u v) (cos v)))\n(define flux (calc/surface-integral F x y z 0 (* 2 3.14159) 0 3.14159))\n(< (abs (- flux (* 4 3.14159))) 0.5)" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "∮_S F · dS = ∫_V div F dV"
     :meta "The invariant is the algebraic essence of the verb; a single counter-example would falsify it and prove the implementation broken. See book-of-math ch15-multivariable-linear-algebra.")
    (:claim "The scalar surface integral of the constant 1 equals the surface's area."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define sphere (lambda (u v) (list (* (cos u) (sin v)) (* (sin u) (sin v)) (cos v))))\n(define one (lambda (x y z) 1))\n(< (abs (- (calc/surface-integral sphere one 'scalar) (* 4 3.14159))) 0.5)" :tags (math program)))
     :invariant "∫∫_S 1 dS = Area(S)"
     :meta "Integrating unity recovers the measure of the surface; this is the defining sanity check that the parametric area element |r_u × r_v| is computed correctly."
     )
    (:claim "Flux of a divergence-free field through a closed surface is zero."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define F (lambda (x y z) (list (- y) x 0)))\n(define sphere (lambda (u v) (list (* (cos u) (sin v)) (* (sin u) (sin v)) (cos v))))\n(< (abs (calc/surface-integral sphere F 'flux)) 0.5)" :tags (cs program)))
     :invariant "div F = 0 ⇒ ∮_S F · dS = 0"
     :meta "By the divergence theorem a solenoidal field has zero net flux through any closed surface; a nonzero result would prove either a nonzero divergence or a broken normal orientation."
     )
    (:eng "The runnable claim doubles as a CI smoke test — if the invariant ever prints #f, the verb has drifted."
     :math "The invariant is the defining property in the calc-nd category."
     :cs "Property-based test: the program above is a runnable falsifier candidate for a QuickCheck-style fuzzer."))
  :row-5-emergence (
   
    (:base "calc/surface-integral — see :summary."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define F (lambda (p) (list (car p) (cadr p) (caddr p))))\n(define x (lambda (u v) (* (cos u) (sin v))))\n(define y (lambda (u v) (* (sin u) (sin v))))\n(define z (lambda (u v) (cos v)))\n(list (calc/surface-integral F x y z 0 (* 2 3.14159) 0 3.14159) (* 4 3.14159))" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "divergence-theorem-witness"
     :meta "The emergent divergence-theorem-witness belongs to the family of composed behaviours where one verb's output feeds the next verb's input, and the pair carries meaning neither verb has alone. Surface integral + divergence theorem is the classical 3D flux computation.")
    (:base "calc/surface-integral paired with calc/divergence to run the divergence theorem."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define F (lambda (p) (list (car p) (cadr p) (caddr p))))\n(define sphere (lambda (u v) (list (* (cos u) (sin v)) (* (sin u) (sin v)) (cos v))))\n(list 'flux (calc/surface-integral sphere F 'flux) 'div-at-center (calc/divergence F (list 0 0 0)))" :tags (math composition)))
     :emergent "gauss-check"
     :meta "Surface flux on the left, pointwise divergence on the right; running both makes the divergence theorem a live identity (flux = ∭ div F, and div of the position field is the constant 3)."
     )
    (:base "calc/surface-integral paired with calc/curl to run Stokes' theorem on a cap."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define F (lambda (x y z) (list (- y) x 0)))\n(define cap (lambda (u v) (list (* (cos u) (sin v)) (* (sin u) (sin v)) (cos v))))\n(list 'curl-flux (calc/surface-integral cap F 'flux) 'curl-z (caddr (calc/curl F (list 0 0 1))))" :tags (cs composition)))
     :emergent "stokes-witness"
     :meta "The flux of curl F through a surface equals the circulation of F around its boundary; computing both the curl-flux and the curl vector demonstrates Stokes' theorem operationally."
     )
    (:eng "The composition is short enough to inline in a callsite yet meaningful enough to name and reuse."
     :math "Under composition, the calc-nd operations close into the divergence-theorem-witness pattern."
     :cs "The emergent divergence-theorem-witness is a first-class named pattern; recognize it once and it reappears everywhere."))
)

(verb
  :name "calc/surface-revolution" :std "Motoi"
  :library "calc"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(calc/surface-revolution f a b) -> number"
  :summary "Computes the surface area when the curve y=f(x) is rotated about the x-axis from a to b."
  :explanation "Rotating a curve around an axis creates a surface (like a vase or barrel). The surface area is given by the formula S = ∫ₐᵇ 2πy √(1 + (dy/dx)²) dx. This verb computes the integral numerically, combining function values and derivative estimates. It appears in engineering (tank capacity), physics (moment of inertia), and design (volume/material estimates)."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(calc/surface-revolution (lambda (x) 1) 0 2)" :note "Rotating y=1 (horizontal line) about x-axis from 0 to 2 gives a cylinder; surface area = 2π·1·2 = 4π ≈ 12.57")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define f (lambda (x) (sqrt x)))\n(calc/surface-revolution f 0 4)" :note "Rotating y=√x from 0 to 4 creates a cone-like surface; computed area is deterministic float")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (compare-solids-of-revolution)\n  (define line (lambda (x) 1))\n  (define cone (lambda (x) (/ x 2)))\n  (define parab (lambda (x) (sqrt x)))\n  (list 'cylinder (calc/surface-revolution line 0 2)\n        'cone (calc/surface-revolution cone 0 2)\n        'parabola (calc/surface-revolution parab 0 2)))\n(compare-solids-of-revolution)" :note "Compares surface areas for different curves rotated about x-axis; cone grows faster than line, parabola curves differently")
  )
  :caveats ("Formula assumes rotation about the x-axis; rotation about y-axis or other axes requires manual transformation" "f(x) must be non-negative on [a,b] for geometric interpretation (negative y is allowed but interpreted as mirror)" "Requires f to be differentiable; near discontinuities or sharp corners, results are unreliable" "Does not handle self-intersecting or discontinuous curves well")
  :drawbacks ("Expensive: requires computing f and f' at many quadrature points" "Sensitive to spikes in |f'(x)|: steep sections dominate the integrand √(1 + f'²)" "If f oscillates rapidly, accuracy can degrade; use higher tolerance or refine mesh" "Does not visualize the surface; must interpret numeric result as area")
  :usecases ("Engineering: computing surface area of tanks, pipes, and pressure vessels" "Physics: calculating moments of inertia and electromagnetic fields on axisymmetric bodies" "Manufacturing: estimating material needed (paint, coating) for rotational parts" "Design optimization: minimizing surface area (cost) subject to volume constraints")
  :related ("calc/volume-revolution" "calc/arc-length" "calc/integral" "calc/derivative")
  :learn (:concept "Surface area element dS = 2πy ds in polar/rotational coordinates; integration of arc length weighted by radius" :prerequisites ("calc/integral" "calc/derivative" "calc/arc-length") :progression "Master surface-revolution, then explore calc/volume-revolution for volume-equivalent solids")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define (paint-needed profile a b coverage-per-m2)\n  (/ (calc/surface-revolution profile a b) coverage-per-m2))\n(paint-needed (lambda (x) 0.5) 0 4 10)" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "(define cone (lambda (x) x))\n(< (abs (- (calc/surface-revolution cone 0 1) (* 3.14159 (sqrt 2)))) 0.01)" :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define (guarded-surface f a b) (if (> b a) (calc/surface-revolution f a b) 'empty-interval))\n(guarded-surface (lambda (x) (sqrt x)) 0 4)" :tags (cs program))))
    (:problem "A cup or lampshade area estimation needs the surface generated by rotating y=f(x) about the x-axis; the audit wants a single integral answer."
     :programs (
       (:code "(calc/surface-revolution (lambda (x) x) 0 1)" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "Surface area of cone y=x, 0≤x≤1 is π√2; operator sees ≈ 4.44."
     :meta "arc-length gives length of a curve; surface-revolution gives area of the surface swept out. Different quantities, both come from the same arc-length integrand.")
    (:eng "The operator gets one line per call; failure surfaces the exact input that broke the expectation."
     :math "Category: calc-1d; the verb operates within its declared algebraic type."
     :cs "Complexity as declared in the signature; deterministic and testable."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "'2D area from a rotating 1D curve'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "calc native" :code "(calc/surface-revolution (lambda (x) x) 0 1)")
       (:domain "scene-mesh-area of a lathe object" :code "(calc/surface-revolution (lambda (x) x) 0 1)")
       (:domain "∫ 2π f(x) √(1 + f\\'(x)²) dx" :code "(calc/surface-revolution (lambda (x) x) 0 1)"))
     :explanation "Rung 1 is calc/surface-revolution in its native library. Rung 2 shows the same ''2D area from a rotating 1D curve'' shape in a metaphor domain. Rung 3 lifts the shape into a math/logical setting where the transfer is formal. Recognizing the same shape in all three lets the reader carry the verb's intuition into problems Motoi does not directly cover."
     :meta "The shape transfers because ''2D area from a rotating 1D curve'' is substrate-independent — the same relationship holds whenever the underlying category admits the required structure.")
    (:eng "Engineers who have met the shape elsewhere already know when to reach for calc/surface-revolution."
     :math "The shape is captured by a small equation set; each rung is a model of that equation set."
     :cs "Once the shape is named, the same algorithm skeleton (traverse, close, compare) applies at each rung."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define line (lambda (x) 2))\n(< (abs (- (calc/surface-revolution line 0 3) (* 2 3.14159 2 3))) 0.01)" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "(define f (lambda (x) x))\n(< (abs (- (+ (calc/surface-revolution f 0 1) (calc/surface-revolution f 1 2))\n           (calc/surface-revolution f 0 2))) 0.05)" :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define (nonneg? x) (>= x 0))\n(nonneg? (calc/surface-revolution (lambda (x) (sqrt x)) 0 4))" :tags (cs program))))
    (:claim "Surface area is non-negative and monotone in the interval [a, b]."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) x))\n(<= (calc/surface-revolution f 0 1) (calc/surface-revolution f 0 2))" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "∀ [a, b] ⊆ [c, d]. sr(f, a, b) ≤ sr(f, c, d)"
     :meta "The invariant is the algebraic essence of the verb; a single counter-example would falsify it and prove the implementation broken. See book-of-math ch14-calculus-2.")
    (:eng "The runnable claim doubles as a CI smoke test — if the invariant ever prints #f, the verb has drifted."
     :math "The invariant is the defining property in the calc-1d category."
     :cs "Property-based test: the program above is a runnable falsifier candidate for a QuickCheck-style fuzzer."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define (material-cost profile a b area-price)\n  (* area-price (calc/surface-revolution profile a b)))\n(map (lambda (p) (material-cost p 0 2 5))\n     (list (lambda (x) 1) (lambda (x) (/ x 2)) (lambda (x) (sqrt x))))" :tags (eng composition))
      (:code "(define f (lambda (x) x))\n(define (area-to-volume-ratio f a b)\n  (/ (calc/surface-revolution f a b) (calc/volume-revolution f a b)))\n(area-to-volume-ratio f 0.5 2)" :tags (math composition))
      (:code "(define (pick-min-surface profiles a b)\n  (let loop ((ps (cdr profiles)) (best (car profiles)) (bv (calc/surface-revolution (car profiles) a b)))\n    (if (null? ps) best\n        (let ((v (calc/surface-revolution (car ps) a b)))\n          (if (< v bv) (loop (cdr ps) (car ps) v) (loop (cdr ps) best bv))))))\n(pick-min-surface (list (lambda (x) 1) (lambda (x) (/ x 2))) 0 2)" :tags (cs composition))))
    (:base "calc/surface-revolution — see :summary."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) x))\n(list (calc/arc-length f 0 1) (calc/surface-revolution f 0 1) (calc/volume-revolution f 0 1))" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "lathe-metric-triple"
     :meta "The emergent lathe-metric-triple belongs to the family of composed behaviours where one verb's output feeds the next verb's input, and the pair carries meaning neither verb has alone. Arc-length + surface-of-revolution + volume-of-revolution together characterize a lathe object.")
    (:eng "The composition is short enough to inline in a callsite yet meaningful enough to name and reuse."
     :math "Under composition, the calc-1d operations close into the lathe-metric-triple pattern."
     :cs "The emergent lathe-metric-triple is a first-class named pattern; recognize it once and it reappears everywhere."))
)

(verb
  :name "calc/tangent-line" :std "Motoi"
  :library "calc"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(calc/tangent-line f a) -> (list slope intercept)"
  :summary "Computes the tangent line to the curve y=f(x) at x=a, returning (slope, intercept) as an a-list."
  :explanation "The tangent line is the best linear approximation to a curve at a point. Its slope is f'(a) and it passes through (a, f(a)), so its equation is y = f'(a)(x − a) + f(a). The verb returns this in slope-intercept form: (m, b) where m = f'(a) and b is the y-intercept. Tangent lines are used to linearize nonlinear functions, initialize iterative solvers (Newton's method), and visualize instantaneous rates of change."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(define f (lambda (x) (* x x)))\n(define tl (calc/tangent-line f 2))\n(list 'slope (car tl) 'intercept (cadr tl))" :note "f(x) = x²; at x=2, f'(2)=4 so slope=4 and the tangent passes through (2,4)")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define f (lambda (x) (sin x)))\n(define (pt) (calc/tangent-line f (/ 3.14159 2)))\n(define tline (pt))\n(list 'tangent-to-sin-at-pi/2 tline)\n(list 'slope (car tline) 'intercept (cadr tline))" :note "sin(x) at x=π/2; f(π/2)=1, f'(π/2)=0, so tangent is horizontal: (0, 1)")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (linear-approx f a dx)\n  (let ((tline (calc/tangent-line f a)))\n    (let ((m (car tline)) (b (cadr tline)))\n      (let ((x-new (+ a dx)))\n        (let ((y-approx (+ (* m x-new) b))\n              (y-actual (f x-new)))\n          (list 'x x-new 'linear-approx y-approx 'actual y-actual 'error (- y-actual y-approx)))))))\n(linear-approx (lambda (x) (math/exp x)) 0 0.1)" :note "Uses tangent line at x=0 to approximate e^0.1; error is small for small dx")
  )
  :caveats ("Returns a-list (list of two elements); destructure as (m . b) or access via car/cadr" "Tangent line is only accurate near a; error grows as you move away from x=a" "If f'(a) is poorly behaved (very large or near zero), tangent may be vertical or horizontal" "Does not detect or warn about corners or discontinuities at a")
  :drawbacks ("Requires computing f(a) and f'(a); not cached, so subsequent queries recompute" "Approximation error is O(Δx²) in distance from a; unusable far from tangent point" "Linear approximation ignores higher-order curvature; for worse accuracy, use Taylor series (calc/taylor)" "Does not handle parameterized or implicit curves directly")
  :usecases ("Newton's method: using tangent line to iteratively find roots" "Linearization: approximating nonlinear models near an operating point (engineering)" "Visualization: drawing tangent vectors on curves in graphing software" "Error analysis: bounding approximation error in scientific computing")
  :related ("calc/derivative" "calc/newton" "calc/taylor" "calc/limit")
  :learn (:concept "Linear approximation and the tangent line as first-order Taylor polynomial" :prerequisites ("calc/derivative") :progression "Understand tangent lines, then use them in calc/newton and extend to calc/taylor for higher-order approximations")
  :row-2-audit (
