(verb
  :name "calc/taylor" :std "Motoi"
  :library "calc"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(calc/taylor f a n) -> list"
  :summary "Compute the Taylor polynomial of degree n for function f about point a, returned as coefficient list with low-order terms first."
  :explanation "A Taylor polynomial approximates a smooth function near a point by matching the function's value and all its derivatives up to a given order. This verb builds the polynomial coefficients: the constant term f(a), then f'(a), f''(a)/2!, etc. The result is a list where index i holds the coefficient of (x−a)^i, making it easy to evaluate near a by walking the list with powers. Essential for numerical approximation, physics simulation, and understanding local behavior of curves."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(define f (lambda (x) (* x x)))\n(calc/taylor f 0 3)\n;; → (0 0 2 0) — x² has zero constant, zero linear, 2 quadratic, zero cubic" :note "Quadratic function around 0 produces exactly its coefficients; higher-order terms vanish.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define (pow x y) (expt x y))\n(define (fold-left f init xs)\n  (let loop ((xs xs) (acc init))\n    (if (null? xs) acc (loop (cdr xs) (f acc (car xs))))))\n(define (sin-approx x)\n  (let ((poly (calc/taylor (lambda (t) (sin t)) 0 5)))\n    (fold-left (lambda (acc i)\n      (+ acc (* (list-ref poly i) (pow x i))))\n      0 (list 0 1 2 3 4 5))))\n(sin-approx 0.5)\n;; ≈ 0.479 (close to sin(0.5) ≈ 0.479)" :note "Build a polynomial evaluator from the Taylor coefficients; 5th-order captures sine well near 0.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (pow x y) (expt x y))\n(define (fold-left f init xs)\n  (let loop ((xs xs) (acc init))\n    (if (null? xs) acc (loop (cdr xs) (f acc (car xs))))))\n(define (adaptive-approx f a x-val orders)\n  ;; Try successive orders, return first that converges well\n  (let loop ((n 1) (prev-val 0))\n    (if (> n orders)\n      prev-val\n      (let* ((poly (calc/taylor f a n))\n             (val (fold-left (lambda (acc i)\n               (+ acc (* (list-ref poly i) (pow (- x-val a) i))))\n               0 (range n))))\n        (if (< (abs (- val prev-val)) 0.000001)\n          val\n          (loop (+ n 1) val))))))\n(adaptive-approx (lambda (t) (exp t)) 0 0.2 10)" :note "Practical strategy: compute Taylor series iteratively until convergence; avoids over-computing unnecessary terms.")
  )
  :caveats ("Accuracy depends on distance from a; polynomial diverges rapidly far from the expansion point." "Numerical differentiation may accumulate rounding error; use higher precision or symbolic input if available." "High-degree polynomials can exhibit Runge's phenomenon (wild oscillation) if not evaluated carefully.")
  :drawbacks ("Linear cost in degree n; impractical for very high orders." "Only good approximation in a neighborhood of a, not globally." "If f is discontinuous or non-smooth, derivatives do not exist and the method fails.")
  :usecases ("Physics: approximate acceleration near a point without expensive function calls." "Numerics: seed Newton's method or other rootfinding with a quadratic model." "Visualization: cheaply sample a smooth curve at many points by caching the Taylor polynomial.")
  :related ("calc/derivative" "calc/tangent-line" "calc/newton")
  :learn (:concept "Local polynomial approximation via derivative values; the foundation of all calculus-based numerical methods." :prerequisites ("calc/derivative") :progression "Apply Taylor series in calc/newton for root-finding, or in calc/arc-length for accurate curvature estimates.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define (taylor-eval f a n x)\n  (let ((cs (calc/taylor f a n)))\n    (let loop ((cs cs) (k 0) (acc 0))\n      (if (null? cs) acc\n          (loop (cdr cs) (+ k 1) (+ acc (* (car cs) (expt (- x a) k))))))))\n(taylor-eval (lambda (x) (math/exp x)) 0 4 0.2)" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "(define cs (calc/taylor (lambda (x) (* x x)) 0 3))\n(and (< (abs (list-ref cs 2)) 2.01) (> (abs (list-ref cs 2)) 1.99))" :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define (taylor-degree f a n) (- (length (calc/taylor f a n)) 1))\n(taylor-degree (lambda (x) (sin x)) 0 5)" :tags (cs program))))
    (:problem "An analytic approximation for a complicated function around a point needs the Taylor polynomial of chosen degree so the caller can trade accuracy for cost."
     :programs (
       (:code "(calc/taylor (lambda (x) (exp x)) 0 3)" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "Third-degree Taylor of exp around 0 is 1 + x + x²/2 + x³/6; operator sees the polynomial coefficients."
     :meta "tangent-line is degree 1; taylor is any degree. Use taylor for higher-order accuracy.")
    (:eng "The operator gets one line per call; failure surfaces the exact input that broke the expectation."
     :math "Category: calc-1d; the verb operates within its declared algebraic type."
     :cs "Complexity as declared in the signature; deterministic and testable."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "'polynomial approximation via derivatives'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "calc native" :code "(calc/taylor (lambda (x) (exp x)) 0 3)")
       (:domain "N-th-order motion prediction" :code "(calc/taylor (lambda (x) (exp x)) 0 3)")
       (:domain "Taylor polynomial T_n(x) = Σ f^{(k)}(a)/k! (x-a)^k" :code "(calc/taylor (lambda (x) (exp x)) 0 3)"))
     :explanation "Rung 1 is calc/taylor in its native library. Rung 2 shows the same ''polynomial approximation via derivatives'' shape in a metaphor domain. Rung 3 lifts the shape into a math/logical setting where the transfer is formal. Recognizing the same shape in all three lets the reader carry the verb's intuition into problems Motoi does not directly cover."
     :meta "The shape transfers because ''polynomial approximation via derivatives'' is substrate-independent — the same relationship holds whenever the underlying category admits the required structure.")
    (:eng "Engineers who have met the shape elsewhere already know when to reach for calc/taylor."
     :math "The shape is captured by a small equation set; each rung is a model of that equation set."
     :cs "Once the shape is named, the same algorithm skeleton (traverse, close, compare) applies at each rung."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define cs (calc/taylor (lambda (x) (math/exp x)) 0 4))\n(< (abs (- (car cs) 1)) 0.001)" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "(define (taylor-eval-at f a n x)\n  (let ((cs (calc/taylor f a n)))\n    (let loop ((cs cs) (k 0) (acc 0))\n      (if (null? cs) acc\n          (loop (cdr cs) (+ k 1) (+ acc (* (car cs) (expt (- x a) k))))))))\n(define f (lambda (x) (sin x)))\n(< (abs (- (taylor-eval-at f 0 5 0) (f 0))) 0.001)" :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define cs (calc/taylor (lambda (x) (* x x x)) 0 5))\n(list? cs)" :tags (cs program))))
    (:claim "The Taylor polynomial of degree n at a agrees with f in value and first n derivatives at a."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) (sin x)))\n(define T (calc/taylor f 0 5))\n(define (eval-poly cs x) (let loop ((cs cs) (k 0) (acc 0)) (if (null? cs) acc (loop (cdr cs) (+ k 1) (+ acc (* (car cs) (expt x k)))))))\n(< (abs (- (eval-poly T 0.1) (f 0.1))) 0.001)" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "∀ k ≤ n. T_n^{(k)}(a) = f^{(k)}(a)"
     :meta "The invariant is the algebraic essence of the verb; a single counter-example would falsify it and prove the implementation broken. See book-of-math ch14-calculus-2.")
    (:eng "The runnable claim doubles as a CI smoke test — if the invariant ever prints #f, the verb has drifted."
     :math "The invariant is the defining property in the calc-1d category."
     :cs "Property-based test: the program above is a runnable falsifier candidate for a QuickCheck-style fuzzer."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define (fast-sampler f a n)\n  (let ((cs (calc/taylor f a n)))\n    (lambda (x)\n      (let loop ((cs cs) (k 0) (acc 0))\n        (if (null? cs) acc\n            (loop (cdr cs) (+ k 1) (+ acc (* (car cs) (expt (- x a) k)))))))))\n(define g (fast-sampler (lambda (x) (math/exp x)) 0 6))\n(map g (list -0.1 0 0.1))" :tags (eng composition))
      (:code "(define (taylor-eval f a n x)\n  (let ((cs (calc/taylor f a n)))\n    (let loop ((cs cs) (k 0) (acc 0))\n      (if (null? cs) acc (loop (cdr cs) (+ k 1) (+ acc (* (car cs) (expt (- x a) k))))))))\n(map (lambda (n) (taylor-eval (lambda (x) (math/exp x)) 0 n 0.5)) (list 1 2 4 8))" :tags (math composition))
      (:code "(define (const-term f a) (car (calc/taylor f a 3)))\n(map (lambda (a) (const-term (lambda (x) (* x x)) a)) (list 0 1 2))" :tags (cs composition))))
    (:base "calc/taylor — see :summary."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define (eval-poly cs x) (let loop ((cs cs) (k 0) (acc 0)) (if (null? cs) acc (loop (cdr cs) (+ k 1) (+ acc (* (car cs) (expt x k)))))))\n(define f (lambda (x) (exp x)))\n(map (lambda (n) (eval-poly (calc/taylor f 0 n) 0.5)) (list 1 2 3 5 10))" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "convergent-approximation"
     :meta "The emergent convergent-approximation belongs to the family of composed behaviours where one verb's output feeds the next verb's input, and the pair carries meaning neither verb has alone. Taylor + refinement is the pattern for controlled-accuracy approximation. See book-of-ml appendix-a for the analytic prereqs.")
    (:eng "The composition is short enough to inline in a callsite yet meaningful enough to name and reuse."
     :math "Under composition, the calc-1d operations close into the convergent-approximation pattern."
     :cs "The emergent convergent-approximation is a first-class named pattern; recognize it once and it reappears everywhere."))
)

(verb
  :name "calc/total-differential" :std "Motoi"
  :library "calc"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(calc/total-differential f a dv) -> number"
  :summary "Approximate the change in f near point a when input moves by displacement dv, using the gradient: df ≈ ∇f(a) · dv."
  :explanation "The total differential extends the single-variable derivative to multivariable functions. Rather than asking 'what is f at a?', it asks 'if we nudge the input by a small vector dv, by how much does f change?' The answer is the dot product of the gradient ∇f at a with dv. This is the best linear approximation to the change, exact to first order. Used everywhere: optimization (finding descent directions), physics (work = force · displacement), and numerical methods (predictor–corrector steps)."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(define f (lambda (xy) (+ (* (car xy) (car xy)) (* (cadr xy) (cadr xy)))))\n;; f is x² + y²; at a = (1, 2), try dv = (0.01, 0.01)\n(calc/total-differential f (list 1 2) (list 0.01 0.01))\n;; ≈ 0.06 (gradient is (2x, 2y) = (2, 4), dot (0.01, 0.01) = 0.06)" :note "Linear approximation predicts change without recomputing f at the new point.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define (surface-height lat lon) (+ (* 0.5 lat) (* 0.3 lon)))\n(define base-pos (list 40.7 -74.0))\n(define step (list 0.001 0.001))\n(define dh (calc/total-differential surface-height base-pos step))\n(display (list \"Height change over small step:\" dh))\n;; \"Height change over small step: \" 0.0008" :note "Estimate elevation change when moving slightly north and east; avoids redundant height lookups.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (gradient-descent f a learning-rate steps)\n  (let loop ((pos a) (i 0))\n    (if (>= i steps)\n      pos\n      (let ((grad (calc/gradient f pos)))\n        (let ((step-vec (map (lambda (g) (* (- learning-rate) g)) grad)))\n          (let ((new-pos (map + pos step-vec)))\n            (loop new-pos (+ i 1))))))))\n;; Or, at each step, use total-differential to estimate new f value before moving:\n(define (approx-step f pos lr)\n  (let ((grad (calc/gradient f pos)))\n    (let ((delta (map (lambda (g) (* (- lr) g)) grad)))\n      (let ((est-df (calc/total-differential f pos delta)))\n        (list (map + pos delta) est-df)))))\n(approx-step (lambda (xy) (+ (* (car xy) (car xy)) (* (cadr xy) (cadr xy))))\n  (list 1.0 1.0) 0.01)" :note "Estimate function decrease before taking a full step; useful for adaptive step sizing or line search.")
  )
  :caveats ("Only accurate for small displacements dv; error grows quadratically with |dv|." "Requires the gradient at a; if f is non-smooth or poorly conditioned, the approximation fails." "If dv is not small, use calc/taylor (2nd-order) or numerical integration instead.")
  :drawbacks ("Linear approximation only; quadratic or higher-order effects are ignored." "Computing the gradient itself requires partial derivatives; cost scales with dimension." "Does not warn if the displacement is too large; you must validate a posteriori.")
  :usecases ("Optimization: gradient descent and Newton's method both rely on total differential reasoning." "Physics: work, energy, and flux calculations all use differential approximations." "Numerical PDE solvers: predictor–corrector schemes use total differential for one-step prediction.")
  :related ("calc/gradient" "calc/derivative" "calc/taylor")
  :learn (:concept "First-order linear approximation in multiple dimensions; the gateway to multivariable calculus and optimization." :prerequisites ("calc/gradient" "calc/derivative") :progression "Combine with calc/taylor for higher-order accuracy, or use iteratively in gradient descent.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define (predict-change f pos step)\n  (calc/total-differential f pos step))\n(predict-change (lambda (xy) (+ (* (car xy) (car xy)) (* (cadr xy) (cadr xy))))\n  (list 1 2) (list 0.01 0.01))" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(< (abs (- (calc/total-differential f (list 1 1) (list 0.01 0.02)) 0.06)) 0.001)" :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define (safe-td f pos step) (if (= (length pos) (length step)) (calc/total-differential f pos step) 'dim-mismatch))\n(safe-td (lambda (p) (* (car p) (cadr p))) (list 2 3) (list 0.1 0.1))" :tags (cs program))))
    (:problem "A change-propagation estimate needs df = Σ ∂f/∂x_i dx_i — the first-order linear response of f to a perturbation."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(calc/total-differential f (list 1 1) (list 0.01 0.02))" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "df ≈ 2·0.01 + 2·0.02 = 0.06; operator sees ≈ 0.06."
     :meta "gradient is ∇f; total-differential applies the gradient to a specific perturbation vector. gradient(f) · dx.")
    (:eng "The operator gets one line per call; failure surfaces the exact input that broke the expectation."
     :math "Category: calc-nd; the verb operates within its declared algebraic type."
     :cs "Complexity as declared in the signature; deterministic and testable."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "'linear-response to a small perturbation'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "calc native" :code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))")
       (:domain "error-propagation over a sensor sum" :code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(calc/total-differential f (list 1 1) (list 0.01 0.02))")
       (:domain "df = ∇f · dx" :code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(calc/total-differential f (list 1 1) (list 0.01 0.02))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1 is calc/total-differential in its native library. Rung 2 shows the same ''linear-response to a small perturbation'' shape in a metaphor domain. Rung 3 lifts the shape into a math/logical setting where the transfer is formal. Recognizing the same shape in all three lets the reader carry the verb's intuition into problems Motoi does not directly cover."
     :meta "The shape transfers because ''linear-response to a small perturbation'' is substrate-independent — the same relationship holds whenever the underlying category admits the required structure.")
    (:eng "Engineers who have met the shape elsewhere already know when to reach for calc/total-differential."
     :math "The shape is captured by a small equation set; each rung is a model of that equation set."
     :cs "Once the shape is named, the same algorithm skeleton (traverse, close, compare) applies at each rung."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(< (abs (calc/total-differential f (list 1 1) (list 0 0))) 0.001)" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(define x (list 1 1))\n(< (abs (+ (calc/total-differential f x (list 0.05 0.05))\n           (calc/total-differential f x (list -0.05 -0.05)))) 0.001)" :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define (number-out? f pos step) (number? (calc/total-differential f pos step)))\n(number-out? (lambda (p) (* (car p) (cadr p))) (list 1 2) (list 0.01 0.01))" :tags (cs program))))
    (:claim "df is linear in the perturbation dx: total-differential(f, x, av + bw) = a·df(v) + b·df(w)."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p)))))\n(define x (list 1 1))\n(define v (list 0.1 0)) (define w (list 0 0.1))\n(< (abs (- (calc/total-differential f x (list 0.2 0.3))\n           (+ (* 2 (calc/total-differential f x v))\n              (* 3 (calc/total-differential f x w))))) 0.01)" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "df(av + bw) = a·df(v) + b·df(w)"
     :meta "The invariant is the algebraic essence of the verb; a single counter-example would falsify it and prove the implementation broken. See book-of-math ch15-multivariable-linear-algebra.")
    (:eng "The runnable claim doubles as a CI smoke test — if the invariant ever prints #f, the verb has drifted."
     :math "The invariant is the defining property in the calc-nd category."
     :cs "Property-based test: the program above is a runnable falsifier candidate for a QuickCheck-style fuzzer."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define (worst-case-error f pos tol)\n  (define dims (length pos))\n  (define (unit i) (map (lambda (j) (if (= i j) tol 0)) (range dims)))\n  (apply + (map (lambda (i) (abs (calc/total-differential f pos (unit i))))\n                (range dims))))\n(worst-case-error (lambda (p) (* (car p) (cadr p))) (list 2 3) 0.01)" :tags (eng composition))
      (:code "(define (line-search-df f pos dir steps)\n  (map (lambda (t)\n         (calc/total-differential f pos (map (lambda (d) (* t d)) dir)))\n       (range steps)))\n(line-search-df (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p))))\n  (list 1 1) (list 0.01 0.01) 4)" :tags (math composition))
      (:code "(define (descent-direction? f pos dir)\n  (< (calc/total-differential f pos dir) 0))\n(descent-direction? (lambda (p) (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p))))\n  (list 1 1) (list -0.1 -0.1))" :tags (cs composition))))
    (:base "calc/total-differential — see :summary."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (p) (* (car p) (cadr p))))\n(define x (list 1 2))\n(define errs (list (list 0.01 0) (list 0 0.01) (list 0.01 0.01)))\n(map (lambda (d) (calc/total-differential f x d)) errs)" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "error-budget"
     :meta "The emergent error-budget belongs to the family of composed behaviours where one verb's output feeds the next verb's input, and the pair carries meaning neither verb has alone. Total differential = first-order error propagation; used in every measurement pipeline.")
    (:eng "The composition is short enough to inline in a callsite yet meaningful enough to name and reuse."
     :math "Under composition, the calc-nd operations close into the error-budget pattern."
     :cs "The emergent error-budget is a first-class named pattern; recognize it once and it reappears everywhere."))
)

(verb
  :name "calc/volume-revolution" :std "Motoi"
  :library "calc"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(calc/volume-revolution f a b axis) -> number"
  :summary "Compute the volume of a solid formed by rotating the curve y=f(x) about the x-axis or y-axis between x=a and x=b, using the disk method."
  :explanation "When you rotate a curve around an axis, you sweep out a 3D solid. Slice the solid perpendicular to the axis, and each slice is a disk (or washer). The disk-method formula integrates the area of each disk: rotating y=f(x) about the x-axis gives V = ∫π[f(x)]² dx from a to b. This verb evaluates that integral numerically. Essential for engineering (modeling tanks, shafts, rotated profiles) and physics (computing moments of inertia)."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(define f (lambda (x) (sqrt x)))\n;; Rotate y=√x from x=0 to x=4 about the x-axis\n(calc/volume-revolution f 0 4 'x)\n;; ≈ 8π (exact answer for this solid)" :note "Simple function; disk method is exact for powers and roots.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define (cone-profile x) (* 0.5 x))  ;; y = 0.5x: cone of height h, base r=0.5h\n(define cone-vol (calc/volume-revolution cone-profile 0 3 'x))\n(display (list \"Cone volume (h=3):\" cone-vol))\n;; ≈ 2.36 (exact: (1/3)πr²h = (1/3)π(1.5)²(3) = 2.25π)" :note "Linear profile generates a cone; use this to verify design dimensions.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (design-tank target-vol height)\n  ;; Find profile f such that rotating f about x-axis gives desired volume\n  ;; Use bisection to search for the scaling factor\n  (let ((base-profile (lambda (x) (* 0.2 (sqrt (+ 1 (* 0.5 x)))))))\n    (let loop ((scale-low 0.5) (scale-high 2.0))\n      (if (< (- scale-high scale-low) 0.001)\n        scale-low\n        (let* ((scale-mid (/ (+ scale-low scale-high) 2.0))\n               (scaled-f (lambda (x) (* scale-mid (base-profile x))))\n               (vol (calc/volume-revolution scaled-f 0 height 'x)))\n          (if (< vol target-vol)\n            (loop scale-mid scale-high)\n            (loop scale-low scale-mid)))))))\n(define tank-scale (design-tank 100 5))\n(display (list \"Tank scale factor:\" tank-scale))" :note "Invert the integral: given a target volume, find the profile that achieves it via bisection.")
  )
  :caveats ("Assumes f(x) ≥ 0 throughout [a, b]; negative values will compute as positive area (distance to axis)." "Only handles solid-of-revolution about one of the two coordinate axes; general axis rotation requires a coordinate change." "Numerical integration error depends on smoothness of f; discontinuities or sharp corners reduce accuracy.")
  :drawbacks ("Quadrature cost scales with required accuracy; smooth functions integrate faster than oscillatory ones." "Disk method is inefficient for certain shapes (e.g., thin shells); washer or shell method may be cheaper for some geometries." "No symbolic output; if exact form is needed, use symbolic integration (outside Motoi).")
  :usecases ("Engineering: tank and pipe design, computing exact volumes from CAD profiles." "Physics: moment of inertia for rotating machinery (I ∝ ∫ r² dm)." "Architecture: dome and barrel-vault volume under a given profile.")
  :related ("calc/integral" "calc/arc-length" "calc/surface-revolution")
  :learn (:concept "The disk method: slicing a solid perpendicular to an axis and integrating cross-sectional areas." :prerequisites ("calc/integral") :progression "Compare with calc/surface-revolution for surface area; learn shell method separately for other geometries.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define (tank-liters profile a b)\n  (* 1000 (calc/volume-revolution profile a b 'x)))\n(tank-liters (lambda (x) (sqrt x)) 0 4 'x)" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "(define cone (lambda (x) x))\n(< (abs (- (calc/volume-revolution cone 0 1 'x) (/ 3.14159 3))) 0.01)" :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define (guarded-volume f a b axis) (if (memq axis '(x y)) (calc/volume-revolution f a b axis) 'bad-axis))\n(guarded-volume (lambda (x) (sqrt x)) 0 4 'x)" :tags (cs program))))
    (:problem "A vessel-capacity computation needs the volume swept out by rotating y=f(x) about the x-axis."
     :programs (
       (:code "(calc/volume-revolution (lambda (x) x) 0 1)" :tags (program)))
     :explanation "Cone y=x, 0≤x≤1 has volume π/3 ≈ 1.047; operator sees ≈ 1.047."
     :meta "surface-revolution gives the area; volume-revolution gives the enclosed volume. Same rotation, different quantity.")
    (:eng "The operator gets one line per call; failure surfaces the exact input that broke the expectation."
     :math "Category: calc-1d; the verb operates within its declared algebraic type."
     :cs "Complexity as declared in the signature; deterministic and testable."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "'3D volume from a rotating 1D curve'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "calc native" :code "(calc/volume-revolution (lambda (x) x) 0 1)")
       (:domain "lathe object volume" :code "(calc/volume-revolution (lambda (x) x) 0 1)")
       (:domain "π ∫ f(x)² dx" :code "(calc/volume-revolution (lambda (x) x) 0 1)"))
     :explanation "Rung 1 is calc/volume-revolution in its native library. Rung 2 shows the same ''3D volume from a rotating 1D curve'' shape in a metaphor domain. Rung 3 lifts the shape into a math/logical setting where the transfer is formal. Recognizing the same shape in all three lets the reader carry the verb's intuition into problems Motoi does not directly cover."
     :meta "The shape transfers because ''3D volume from a rotating 1D curve'' is substrate-independent — the same relationship holds whenever the underlying category admits the required structure.")
    (:eng "Engineers who have met the shape elsewhere already know when to reach for calc/volume-revolution."
     :math "The shape is captured by a small equation set; each rung is a model of that equation set."
     :cs "Once the shape is named, the same algorithm skeleton (traverse, close, compare) applies at each rung."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define cyl (lambda (x) 2))\n(< (abs (- (calc/volume-revolution cyl 0 3 'x) (* 3.14159 4 3))) 0.01)" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "(define f (lambda (x) x))\n(< (abs (- (+ (calc/volume-revolution f 0 1 'x) (calc/volume-revolution f 1 2 'x))\n           (calc/volume-revolution f 0 2 'x))) 0.05)" :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define (nonneg? x) (>= x 0))\n(nonneg? (calc/volume-revolution (lambda (x) (sqrt x)) 0 4 'x))" :tags (cs program))))
    (:claim "Volume is non-negative and monotone in the interval [a, b]."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) x))\n(<= (calc/volume-revolution f 0 1) (calc/volume-revolution f 0 2))" :tags (program)))
     :invariant "∀ [a, b] ⊆ [c, d]. vr(f, a, b) ≤ vr(f, c, d)"
     :meta "The invariant is the algebraic essence of the verb; a single counter-example would falsify it and prove the implementation broken. See book-of-math ch14-calculus-2.")
    (:eng "The runnable claim doubles as a CI smoke test — if the invariant ever prints #f, the verb has drifted."
     :math "The invariant is the defining property in the calc-1d category."
     :cs "Property-based test: the program above is a runnable falsifier candidate for a QuickCheck-style fuzzer."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:programs (
      (:code "(define (fits-budget? profile a b max-vol)\n  (<= (calc/volume-revolution profile a b 'x) max-vol))\n(fits-budget? (lambda (x) (sqrt x)) 0 4 30)" :tags (eng composition))
      (:code "(define f (lambda (x) x))\n(define (surface-to-volume f a b)\n  (/ (calc/surface-revolution f a b) (calc/volume-revolution f a b 'x)))\n(surface-to-volume f 0.5 2)" :tags (math composition))
      (:code "(define (volume-scan profiles a b)\n  (map (lambda (p) (calc/volume-revolution p a b 'x)) profiles))\n(volume-scan (list (lambda (x) 1) (lambda (x) x) (lambda (x) (sqrt x))) 0 2)" :tags (cs composition))))
    (:base "calc/volume-revolution — see :summary."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define f (lambda (x) x))\n(list (calc/arc-length f 0 1) (calc/surface-revolution f 0 1) (calc/volume-revolution f 0 1))" :tags (composition)))
     :emergent "lathe-metric-triple"
     :meta "The emergent lathe-metric-triple belongs to the family of composed behaviours where one verb's output feeds the next verb's input, and the pair carries meaning neither verb has alone. Arc-length + surface + volume = lathe geometry triple; used together in CAD.")
    (:eng "The composition is short enough to inline in a callsite yet meaningful enough to name and reuse."
     :math "Under composition, the calc-1d operations close into the lathe-metric-triple pattern."
     :cs "The emergent lathe-metric-triple is a first-class named pattern; recognize it once and it reappears everywhere."))
)

